{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1523019-princess-the-hopeful-crystal-edition","id":1523019,"title":"Princess: the Hopeful, Crystal Edition","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-09-23T06:36:04-07:00","id":1523019,"title":"Princess: the Hopeful, Crystal Edition","content":"Princess: the Hopeful is a fanmade game line about magical girls in the Chronicles of Darkness, struggling against the overwhelming evil with the light of Hope. A lot of people loved the premise but found the rules clunky and the execution profoundly unfocused. I was one of them.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1foHT2j05pzFth0FFPBTOJ4lTw3X4X2-10rjqBeJkK1A/\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: The Hopeful, Crystal Edition</a><br /> <br /> It's not DONE done -- it's in beta, which means it's feature-complete. A few options need to be filled in (a couple stat blocks, some Signets only have one option at a given level, Embassies don't have all their benefits, and not all the interstitial fictions are in), but all of the systems are in and you can play it perfectly fine. I mean, it's probably imbalanced as all hell, but that's what a beta is for. It's also probably formatted badly, and there's places I didn't catch where I use the wrong term for something because I changed it later. That's why it's only 0.8.<br /> <br /> Crystal Edition is not a page-one rewrite of P:tH, but it's at least a page 2 or 3 rewrite. The structure and Queens are still there but everything has been revamped and refocused to give it clear through-lines both emotionally and physically. And the magic system is totally new and, though I do say so myself, it's <i>really</i> cool.<br /> <br /> There's no more specific spells to buy one at a time. Each Charm has a basic action to it, and a bunch of Upgrades that expand its capabilities multiplicatively, so you stack together your Upgrades to get a spell that can do something neither Upgrade alone could do, and in doing so make an anime word salad spell name! Do you want to hit a ghost with a truck? Give it a Redline Phantom Step! Fling someone's bullets back at them? Rewinding Restoration, Stat! You wanna do sick air combos with multiple weapons? Weapon Master into Aerial Rave! Play three-card monte with your own presence? Blunt Tricky Cunning Illusion! How about a devastating magical finisher? BOTTOMLESS INFINITY PETADRIVE ANNIHILATION HYPER-EXPLOSIVE <b>BUSTER</b>!<br /> <br /> Story and theme wise, The All-Consuming Darkness is not some new and distinct entity that inflicts supernatural taint on people to make them Darkened when they spend too long near a tainted place. The taint of the Darkness is <i>fear</i>. The supernatural corruptive force that drives others to spread misery in its name is literally the emotion of fear. The game's central thesis is about how much of the suffering we inflict on others has fear at its heart, how fear offers an ultimatum of &quot;spread me to others and I will stop tormenting you for a moment.&quot; The world is drenched in the Darkness and thus drenched in fear down to its bones, and Princesses believe that all of the weird-ass inexplicable nonsensical spooky horrors of the CofD are all manifestations of the Darkness, though of course whether or not they are correct about that is another matter.<br /> <br /> The basic &quot;okay so what do I do&quot; problem is solved by presenting a basic ground-level threat: Nightscapes, which are like Madoka labyrinths or Persona dungeons created by someone breaking under the weight of fear, and send out monsters to re-enact it. You don't just need to blow up the monsters, you need to figure out the source of the pain and overcome the psychodrama the prisoner has created in order to solve it, so there's multiple types of gameplay involved.<br /> <br /> Instead of having a focus on virtue and public service that got frankly weird in the old versions, a magical girl character is someone who has a vividly defined personality and ideals, such that when they overcome obstacles, it's validation of their ideals. Princesses now have a Drive and Dread instead of Virtue and Vice; a guiding principle that they're About and something whose fear most motivates their behavior. Wisps are regained from spending Willpower, your human spirit purifying and refining. You can emotionally invest yourself into whatever you do by Flaring your power, granting you a free dot of your power stat, Inner Light, and you can do it multiple times... but if you emotionally commit like that, and you still fail, or you still have to compromise your Belief, the consequences will be devastating. The stakes are high and the tension is overwhelming and you will need to devote everything you are to succeeding and that <i>might still not be enough</i>. Rather than uncertainty of if you're a human any more, the driving uncertainty is if you are good enough, if anyone could possibly be good enough, and what happens when you fail. Camaraderies are gone, coordinating information through the Dreamlands is next to impossible, there's no extant support structure for Princesses -- it's all on your own shoulders. <br /> <br /> I've spent the better part of a year on this, with help from the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/XRUcGgd\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful Discord</a>, and I think the result is really, really good. Check it out if the premise even vaguely interests you, I'd love to get as much feedback as I can, I want this to be the best book it can possibly be.\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2},{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28206-huitzil37","name":"Huitzil37","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-23T08:04:11-07:00","id":1523029,"title":"","content":"Alexa play &quot;I Am Moana&quot; from the Moana soundtrack cause i know I'll be reading today!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-23T20:01:13-07:00","id":1523063,"title":"","content":"Still reading, but would like to point out that the way the Soulless and Enervated Conditions work mean there is currently no point to the mechanics for what comes after Belief 0 unless that character has a Vice through the Vice-Ridden Merit, because losing Willpower due to the Soulless Condition is a function of indulging your Vice, not time alone; you've specified that the Fallen lose their Dread once they're no longer transformed, which means without a specific Merit they can't lose <i>any</i> of their Willpower dots, let alone their last.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-24T13:55:27-07:00","id":1523099,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1523019-princess-the-hopeful-crystal-edition?p=1523063#post1523063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Still reading, but would like to point out that the way the Soulless and Enervated Conditions work mean there is currently no point to the mechanics for what comes after Belief 0 unless that character has a Vice through the Vice-Ridden Merit, because losing Willpower due to the Soulless Condition is a function of indulging your Vice, not time alone; you've specified that the Fallen lose their Dread once they're no longer transformed, which means without a specific Merit they can't lose <i>any</i> of their Willpower dots, let alone their last.</div> </div> </div> </div>Noted.<br /> <br /> Any opinions on how it works overall?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28206-huitzil37","name":"Huitzil37","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-24T22:02:12-07:00","id":1523118,"title":"","content":"Caveat that I'm still in the process of reading, having just reached the start of the Signet Charms section today, but so far\u2026<br /> <br /> The degree to which Belief is easier to retain and regain than other purchased Integrity analogues makes me wonder why it isn't running on a costless model instead.<br /> <br /> The general tone of the text seems more interested in being nominally a magical girl game that tells you it's set in the Chronicles of Darkness than actually engaging with what &quot;magical girls in the Chronicles of Darkness&quot; could mean. An argument could be made that the Noble are coming at the supernatural from the same prioritized angle as werewolves, deviants, and hunters such that even among the court of the Queen of Currents there aren't many occultist-adjacent Princesses who're curious about what the Inner Light is in the greater scheme of things and the metaphysical nitty-gritty of their condition, but when Princesses have been around for at least long enough to have reached middle age it feels off for the setting presentation to include discussion of the rough &quot;timeline&quot; of the fall of the Fearless Kingdoms not any discussion of how they've moved beyond the most simplistic declarative statements about how the world works.<br /> <br /> Speaking of which, I don't feel that the presented history and metaphysics of The All-Consuming Darkness as the actual emotion of fear (somehow distinct from the heuristic of caution in a world where monsters still existed and things still went wrong and people died and all manner of normal things to be afraid of) hangs together at all. Survival is hard; growth and development and community require directed work; utopia is a process and it feels cheap to claim that &quot;the best of all possible worlds&quot; was achieved without anyone ever fearing for their life along the way <i>and simultaneously</i> that the Noble being reincarnated from such a world are not meaningfully psychologically different from the people they grew up with.<br /> <br /> The overall flat structure of Charms and Upgrades feels a little fiddly \u2014 there's a lot of Charms that are basically modeling minor subsystems of their own even before bringing Upgrades into it, and the language model for some of those subsystems feels like it's eating more wordcount than necessary to communicate the mechanical effect and its intended feel, which, combined with Upgrades themselves being explicitly designed to be able to be stacked onto each other for complex and partially freeform effects, gives the impression that there must surely be more mechanically and stylistically elegant ways to accomplish the intended outcomes; Mage has its spell factors and Practices and Arcana (and Down and Dirty Spellcasting mechanics for if &quot;do I pull off this narrative effect&quot; is the most important mechanical question), Deviant has Magnitude and Deviations and keywords affecting Variations at a level that nevertheless changes slowly enough for your powers to be largely consistent from chapter to chapter, Geist 2e makes Keys largely optional motific power-boosts that don't act as gates for new Enhancements, and so on, but if I drop the three Experiences for my Grace to learn how to Detect I basically have to memorize or cross-reference a spell-grammar and a specificity-based range adjustment and a list of modifiers (applicable on-the-fly) that gets longer and more complicated if I invest more into it.<br /> <br /> Almost none of the Charms except Buster have a basic dicepool that doesn't change, Plea's twist on the Social Maneuvering rules is a headache to parse out, Restoration's limits on piecemeal healing add another layer of bookkeeping that really isn't necessary, Anthem explicitly ignores the Clash of Wills system for a category of power it should logically just Clash with where appropriate, and there doesn't seem to be any rhyme or reason to how many other Upgrades are needed to be able to buy a given advanced Upgrade, nor does the book actually seem to spell out whether Charms can be used without being Transformed (there's language that suggests this to be the case, but the breadth of bonuses from Transformed Attributes and Skills and Regalia could as readily mean they're just a lot easier to use in that state). Coupled with the explicit statement that Charms and Upgrades can do some markedly nonstandard stuff (beyond multiple Upgrades outright changing or inverting the basic effect of the Charm), it all starts to look like a bit of a hodgepodge for a power system whose root differentiating structure is essentially verb-based.<br /> <br /> There is a general issue of seemingly making dicepools very large that is giving me vibes of the old RPGnet discussions coming up around the game borrowing a lot of sensibilities and aesthetics from Exalted 2e; I can understand the notion of sincerity as a theme, and there's certainly something to be said for giving every Princess affinity for the &quot;big finishing move&quot; Charm, but I've seen enough anime to know that literally shouting translated six-kanji confections of attack names is a presentational quirk rather than a diegetic phenomenon and I think the multiple Charms with explicit exemptions to that means you do, too.<br /> <br /> While I understand the notion of making a gameline where playable character types are broader than the explicit archetypal basis from media, I can't say I get why Devil May Cry gets more play than Kingdom Hearts on the inspirational media front, nor how the repeated suggestion of 3D glasses as Regalia is supposed to be taken. Even disregarding the divergence from Chronicles' &quot;house style&quot; in the writing, the tone feels all over the place and more than a little over-memed in a way that doesn't help me sell this book to a hypothetical friend who is interested in playing magical girls in the Chronicles of Darkness instead of, say, a very particular game of Deviant.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-26T14:42:48-07:00","id":1523187,"title":"","content":"Thank you very much for the detailed feedback! I am always looking for negative feedback, and I consider it all valuable, even if I don't end up agreeing with it. I would quote reply to each point line by line, but that would take a billion pages.<br /> <br /> I don't get what you mean by Belief being easier to retain. There is a load-bearing core mechanic of &quot;stack this buff that gives you a penalty to all Compromise rolls&quot; and you can roll Compromises for failing, even and especially when you had no other option. Buying with XP is not the primary way you're supposed to get it back, but I figured leaving it in would be useful in a few edge cases and wouldn't hurt anything to have the option. Why do you think it's easier to retain?<br /> <br /> Fear is explicitly distinct from caution or wariness in the setting chapter. I made this clearer in that section, used the phrase &quot;fear as we know it&quot; to help emphasize this is not the way we're used to things working, and added to the glossary section: fear is not the emotion of being concerned for one's safety, it's the thing that tortures you when there might be a concern for your safety, paralyzes you from doing something about it, and demands you inflict pain on others to make itself go away. <br /> <br /> I don't understand what you mean by &quot;a magical girl game that tells you it's set in the Chronicles of Darkness&quot; vs &quot;magical girls in the Chronicles of Darkness.&quot; If you mean atmosphere and tone, I talked in the introduction about how the setting doesn't seem like it should have magical girls, and that's what every magical girl setting is like, because a magical girl story is fundamentally a conflict about what genre the story is. Do you mean it's not dark enough and focuses too much on being cool and empowering? Is that something that darker interstitial fiction could help?<br /> <br /> Or do you mean that it doesn't engage enough with existing canonical setting elements? Because I intentionally took most of those out, those are crossover things and the game needs to stand on its own independently. Also when you talk about the other established setting things, 90% of the time it's Mage, I noticed that and just started tearing pieces out.<br /> <br /> Transformation is required to use Charms unless you have Regalia that lets you use it while not Transformed. This is one of those editing things where I moved stuff and rephrased it and didn't notice the only place I actually stated you have to be Transformed to use them was the section on Luminous Beats. Fixed that. Same deal with the 3D glasses: I just copied it from one section document to the other and didn't notice I didn't take it out.<br /> <br /> The Inner Light in the grand scheme of things is not an external power source you tap into, it's a thing inside of you. Like &quot;using black magic to suck out someone's juices&quot; and &quot;intimidating and browbeating someone into giving you your way&quot; are on the same continuum of &quot;using the Darkness for power,&quot; your magic is on the same continuum as your ability to aspire to mundane things and then make them happen. I definitely could have made that clearer, but don't know if I should. The reason there's no occultism into the Inner Light is, in addition to the notion that it is *extremely* hard for the Radiant to coordinate and communicate, it doesn't fit the metaphor of what magic is. It's part of your transformation, which is discovering your potential and growth and who you want to be. Therefore, it can't be occult, because it cannot be &quot;hidden&quot; from you by any external power and the information can't lie elsewhere. You don't learn Charms by rote, you develop them as your own skills -- where would be a good place to mention this, and how would be a good way to phrase it?<br /> <br /> I erred on the side of wordy in the Upgrades descriptions for the sake of clarity (my thought was, like Magic cards are worded). I find a few more words that make it exactly clear when things end and suchlike are less complicated than being shorter but vaguer. I feel like here's the most intractable disagreement, because you just seem to object to the fundamental elements of a modular system, which is of course going to allow you to increase complexity with each upgrade. <br /> <br /> My experience and opinion have been counter to yours on how complex the system is. I've specifically been told Detect is the clearest and easiest to grasp Charm and the place where the modular Upgrades system really shines. I don't find that all the potential Skill and Attribute combinations you have are unnecessarily complicated, because IME, you pick the ones that give you A Good Dice Pool and that's it. The reason I had the Upgrades allow you to use different Skills or Attributes is to A: feel more like it's Your Thing and not a spell off of a book, and B: since the Attributes you favor are dictated by your Y-splat but the Charms you have affinity for are dictated by your X-splat, you need a way to make every Calling's Charms use at least some of the Attributes each Queen is good at. A Grace of Spades should exist, but if they can't use Manipulation to cast Plea instead of Presence, then they aren't getting to do their thing in their way. <br /> <br /> The dice pools can be a bit high -- it's not a huge number of bonuses since you're picking your best Attributes and Skills instead of stacking them all, but you are getting equipment bonuses and buffs along with it. That's why none of the Charms are pass/fail, and everything scales with Intensity, so it's not a matter of &quot;well I have all the dice ever so I never fail.&quot;<br /> <br /> But ultimately, whether the system is too complicated and fiddly, too wordy, or too dice-y isn't really something we can hash out. I am open to the possibility it might be, but it's the kind of thing we need to see people play in order to see how it shakes out. That's the point of releasing a beta.<br /> <br /> Anthem's Clash is specifically so you can amp it up when usually you can't do that with a Clash. If you rock harder, you should be able to blast away harder magic. Plea I admit is a bit clunky, but I didn't see any other way to do what it needed to do (which is to engage with existing systems in a way that gives it several hooks for Upgrades to go instead of bypassing the system, serve as a way to deal damage to Nightscapes, and very clearly be an &quot;ask very nicely&quot; power and not a &quot;mind control&quot; power). You didn't hit Nightscapes yet, so maybe knowing that &quot;it's the same thing as dealing Psyche damage to a Nightscape&quot; would help. Restoration's re-cast is an artifact of when it worked differently, so I fixed that.<br /> <br /> As to why I included DMC, I explained that in the &quot;inspirations&quot; section, I think it fits in really nicely both in terms of &quot;power of emotions makes you stronger than evil&quot; and &quot;how you fight is an expression of who you are,&quot; it gives a bit of a hook for people who need help figuring out how to make a character concept that isn't the most typical magical girl, and it <i>really</i> opened up some needed character space. Once you say Dante is a follower of Spades and Vergil a follower of Mirrors, who are really some of the best examples to use, why not go whole hog? The reason I didn't use Kingdom Hearts is, well, I have no idea what the fuck is going on in Kingdom Hearts. &quot;Darkness&quot; and &quot;Light&quot; are super important in KH but are <i>very</i> poorly defined, which is not what I need when I'm about vividly defined characters whose actions are validations of their ideals; and the other concepts I know about are either already covered, or would take lots of focus and are too specific (stuff like Nobodies) <br /> <br /> Again, thank you so much! You caught some things I needed to change right away, made me ask some other questions I needed to ask, even the things I didn't agree with are things I should be aware of, and just really I appreciate you taking the time to provide such detailed feedback.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28206-huitzil37","name":"Huitzil37","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-26T21:14:14-07:00","id":1523197,"title":"","content":"Have gotten up to the start of the Dark Magical Girls chapter, so I'm working on slightly more information but haven't had cause to retract any of my earlier impressions. Gonna try to address bits as I can as well as bring in the stuff that further reading has brought to mind:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Huitzil37</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1523019-princess-the-hopeful-crystal-edition?p=1523187#post1523187\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't get what you mean by Belief being easier to retain. There is a load-bearing core mechanic of &quot;stack this buff that gives you a penalty to all Compromise rolls&quot; and you can roll Compromises for failing, even and especially when you had no other option. Buying with XP is not the primary way you're supposed to get it back, but I figured leaving it in would be useful in a few edge cases and wouldn't hurt anything to have the option. Why do you think it's easier to retain?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's split up in a three-tiered structure not unlike Wisdom and Hunter Integrity, but you start at 8, the base dicepools to avoid losing dots are set at 7/5/3 dice, and the Circle Merit's benefits include explicitly converting an automatic loss into a roll and letting you come back from the turbo-strength Soullessness of losing your last dot \u2014 as a reminder of the dice math for the Storytelling System, at four dice you have a roughtly 75% chance of success. Flaring your Belief applies a cumulative penalty, yes, but that's singular cumulative dice and the lower your Belief is the less of a penalty you can actually get out of that (setting aside the need for escalating difficulty or investment with each bonus dot of Inner Light), particularly given that Belief otherwise does not affect the effectiveness of your powers until you bottom out.<br /> <br /> All of this on top of the Experience cost being the lowest the system allows (short of maybe giving you back Beats for buying it) and there being no prerequisite conditions for purchasing Belief makes it seem like there is no readily apparent reason one would have to buy dots instead of maintaining a Circle and Putting It All On The Line for freebies if you have occasion to need to get more dots.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't understand what you mean by &quot;a magical girl game that tells you it's set in the Chronicles of Darkness&quot; vs &quot;magical girls in the Chronicles of Darkness.&quot; If you mean atmosphere and tone, I talked in the introduction about how the setting doesn't seem like it should have magical girls, and that's what every magical girl setting is like, because a magical girl story is fundamentally a conflict about what genre the story is. Do you mean it's not dark enough and focuses too much on being cool and empowering? Is that something that darker interstitial fiction could help?<br /> <br /> Or do you mean that it doesn't engage enough with existing canonical setting elements? Because I intentionally took most of those out, those are crossover things and the game needs to stand on its own independently. Also when you talk about the other established setting things, 90% of the time it's Mage, I noticed that and just started tearing pieces out.<br /> <br /> The Inner Light in the grand scheme of things is not an external power source you tap into, it's a thing inside of you. Like &quot;using black magic to suck out someone's juices&quot; and &quot;intimidating and browbeating someone into giving you your way&quot; are on the same continuum of &quot;using the Darkness for power,&quot; your magic is on the same continuum as your ability to aspire to mundane things and then make them happen. I definitely could have made that clearer, but don't know if I should. The reason there's no occultism into the Inner Light is, in addition to the notion that it is *extremely* hard for the Radiant to coordinate and communicate, it doesn't fit the metaphor of what magic is. It's part of your transformation, which is discovering your potential and growth and who you want to be. Therefore, it can't be occult, because it cannot be &quot;hidden&quot; from you by any external power and the information can't lie elsewhere. You don't learn Charms by rote, you develop them as your own skills -- where would be a good place to mention this, and how would be a good way to phrase it? </div> </div> </div>Mummy is helpful for making some of the point I'm trying to illustrate, so I'll be referencing it as I go here:<br /> <br /> What you have described as the MO of fear-as-defined-as-the-Darkness is the Law of Suffering, which is part of Mummy's general thematic tilt toward pain (and retreating from or pushing through it). The forty-two asshole demiurges of that perspective on the setting inflict it with a glance because the forces of creation were desperate to deny the Devourer her ultimate goal of bringing the world to a final endstate, and the forty-third great god who's the reason the world didn't throw itself into Ammut's maw in despair at the state of things (the forty-two asshole demiurges being the main lasting byproducts of attempting to make a world that will never be &quot;still and silent forever&quot; as a whole) generally stands for self-knowledge and the possibility of transcending the dog-eat-dog state of affairs the Law of Suffering represents.<br /> <br /> As this relates to the setting in general, Chronicles is a game where everybody is flawed and there's weirdness everywhere \u2014 even if you set aside Mage and Beast in their niche as dedicated splats for going out to poke the myriad strangeness of the world, Vampire and Deviant are built around the sheer variety of disconnected ways you can arrive at their respective monster-states, to say nothing of Hunter \u2014 and &quot;everywhere&quot; includes inside the soul, hence why self-knowledge and investigation into the way one relates to the people and the world around them can lead to some characters developing supernatural powers in the process of looking into their own interiority. Especially if you are presenting a game where player characters are still in the process of growing up, no external actor need be involved with the occlusion of things that can help you to know more about yourself and the world you live in.<br /> <br /> Broadly speaking, Chronicles characters having Vices is part of the continuum of recognizing that the world is on some level hostile to life in ways that are unpleasant, momentary comfort can be gotten if you're willing to hurt other people and/or pawn your problems off on Future You, and the pursuit of various forms of power makes doing those things easier (P.S. forms of power not a lot of people know about or recognize have particular utility on this front). The God-Machine maintains a status quo where this remains true. The Exarchs cultivate specific forms of human tyranny on this foundation. The Law of Suffering is as close to a direct overarching metaphysical statement about why the world is like this as there is, attributed to the Judges of Life as a response to the entropic goddess of oblivion who is the hunger eating at reality from within and without.<br /> <br /> Chronicles has compelling characters or categories of super-monster that hook into this phenomenon, and it makes Being An Occultist a loosely moral risk mostly on the strength of the same themes that let us compare the societies of supernatural creatures to organized crime, but also it's a weird old world and there's wonder to be had in it (which is part of the internal conflict changelings deal with coming back from Arcadia and seeing all the beautiful strangeness they couldn't before) and, while &quot;they who fight monsters&quot; is obviously less of a desirable thematic beat for Princess than Hunter (the Twilight Queens' courts notwithstanding), &quot;knowing how monsters and magic work, including what causes the frogs in town to croak a song that makes it rain&quot; seems like a weird lacuna in the Hopeful's aspirational knowledge base if we are to believe they inhabit the same sort of setting the monsters and monster-hunters and investigators do, even without the developmental curiosities of &quot;does the Light have objective morals, and if so, what are they?&quot; or &quot;do different Oaths affect how effective a Princess is in fighting the Darkness?&quot; or &quot;if the Light doesn't have objective morals, can it change as Princesses grapple with and reaffirm their Belief?&quot; Alhambra's floated in places as having the most knowledge of the supernatural among the Noble, but it should by no means be the <i>only</i> place in magical girl society where theory of magic circulates.<br /> <br /> The implicit sort of incuriosity behind such an exclusion despite having as much reason to look into it as hunters, changelings, Sin-Eaters, and Prometheans do couples with other unfortunate presentational elements of the Darkness to flatten the impact of telling us this is a game about magical girls in the Chronicles of Darkness; when we are told that the Darkness doesn't have thoughts and its spawn don't have Aspirations (to such a degree that a different systems term is used for a limited but otherwise identical mechanic), when the things to which the archetypal title of Dark General are applied are philosophical zombies degenerating into unaware caricatures of their former selves who are simply modeled as Horrors who get Command Darkspawn for free, when the people who exploit the Darkness are currently exemplified as petty thugs and wannabe warlocks, <i>and</i> when the Darkness is the source of all monsters in some way as well as nigh-impossible to detect supernaturally save by Sensitivity, and definitions of Integrity and the soul are specifically different from the rest of the franchise, it looks like the game wants to put the Darkness front and center as the focal supernatural framework of Princess while also emphasizing that the Darkness is not interesting save as the ultimate cause of a given source of moral outrage for the Noble to fight against.<br /> <br /> Other gamelines give us tyrants and conspiracies and primeval hungers made manifest making the world a worse place to secure their place in it or progress their apocalyptic plans, but Princess as presented here gives us a blind-idiot Augustinian evil that doesn't seem to even have subverted anything more unusual than bluebook Horrors, and it doesn't seem interested in examining the human contribution to that force's advancement outside the realm of caricature. I feel as though currently I cannot reasonably point to anything within the game's roster of the forces of Darkness as the tool to use for modeling the Big Bad of e.g. any given series of Pretty Cure, which seems like an inherent flaw for a game attempting to model conflict-oriented magical girl stories of any kind. (Possibly the Dark Magical Girls chapter will bring me some new information about the Twilight Queens and their Princesses that changes this impression, but six chapters in and past the section about the forces of Darkness feels like a bad place to get that turnaround).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I erred on the side of wordy in the Upgrades descriptions for the sake of clarity (my thought was, like Magic cards are worded). I find a few more words that make it exactly clear when things end and suchlike are less complicated than being shorter but vaguer. I feel like here's the most intractable disagreement, because you just seem to object to the fundamental elements of a modular system, which is of course going to allow you to increase complexity with each upgrade.<br /> <br /> My experience and opinion have been counter to yours on how complex the system is. I've specifically been told Detect is the clearest and easiest to grasp Charm and the place where the modular Upgrades system really shines. I don't find that all the potential Skill and Attribute combinations you have are unnecessarily complicated, because IME, you pick the ones that give you A Good Dice Pool and that's it. The reason I had the Upgrades allow you to use different Skills or Attributes is to A: feel more like it's Your Thing and not a spell off of a book, and B: since the Attributes you favor are dictated by your Y-splat but the Charms you have affinity for are dictated by your X-splat, you need a way to make every Calling's Charms use at least some of the Attributes each Queen is good at. A Grace of Spades should exist, but if they can't use Manipulation to cast Plea instead of Presence, then they aren't getting to do their thing in their way. </div> </div> </div>I greatly enjoy Deviant, one of the most modular Chronicles games currently published. I assure you that modularity is not the issue I am experiencing with the presentation of Charm Upgrade mechanics.<br /> <br /> My issues stem from Upgrades being in a big loose half-differentiated pile that's hard to keep track of without direct reference and using advancement space where the starting point would have been better-served \u2014 if the idea is for Charms to have dicepools that best suit the Princess that's using them and they aren't learned by rote, why give them a fixed base dicepool at all instead of a set of baseline options to pick from? Should the Upgrades you get for free from the Queens be understood as different in any other way from the rest of the Upgrades? Is there no way to reduce the mental stack of remembering what all fifteen Upgrades for a given Charm can do by subdividing the listings in some way?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You didn't hit Nightscapes yet, so maybe knowing that &quot;it's the same thing as dealing Psyche damage to a Nightscape&quot; would help. </div> </div> </div>Having read the Nightscapes mechanics it is my great regret to tell you that it does not help. Nightscapes are a narratively interesting thing to deal with, but the mechanics have a number of general issues.<br /> <br /> If a Nightscape does not track separate damage types by default, it should probably not have a damage track that shares a name with the ephemeral being Trait that does, especially when earlier their Traits are compared explicitly to those of ephemeral beings. Similarly, it is probably unwise to label the row for extra damage track boxes in the Potency chart with an existing systems term that describes a different type of mechanical effect. (Tangentially, the initial mechanical description of Nightscapes also says they have Power, Finesse, and Resistance traits, but the actual section for those mechanics switches to Brief Nightmare dicepools.)<br /> <br /> If the Enigma track is tracked separately for every group of investigators, it is not a damage track for the Nightscape so much as a rating that needs to be overcome by the investigator.<br /> <br /> It seems inadvisable for the mechanics for Distance and Piercing Through to allow characters sporting Transformed dicepools to extended-action their way to clearing the Psyche track without ever getting emotionally closer to the Prisoner than the supporting cast. (Also, if a human Attribute is resisting your attempts to change the Prisoner's mind about staying in the Nightscape, the most appropriate first suggestion is more likely Resolve than Intelligence, because that's the stat for being stubborn.)<br /> <br /> It does not seem to be stated what happens if the Nightscape runs out of Essence, which makes it hard to know what its per-day Essence consumption by Potency is supposed to tell the reader about it besides the ostensible need to replenish itself.<br /> <br /> Given that ephemeral beings already model being hard to permanently kill by the need to deplete their Corpus and Essence (and spirits gain Willpower based on the amount of Essence they've taken), I cannot help but feel it would be more mechanically elegant to provide ways of depleting its Essence and Willpower instead of giving them three healthbars along with Essence; Essence can probably fill the role Enigma currently serves, and Willpower seems thematically appropriate to take over for Psyche.<br /> <br /> Speaking of which, it feels like there is a lot of reinventing the wheel in the mechanics for Nightmares and I'm not sure what there is to gain from making whole different Manifestation Conditions that mostly do the same thing as Materialize, Fetter, Open, etc.<br /> <br /> And on the narrative front I would be remiss if I didn't link back to the presentation issues with the Darkness to point out that the Tension Conditions are neat compelling narrative beats to hit that are nevertheless most satisfyingly applied as obstacles to helping a character confront an unpleasant truth about themself (and therefore get a better grasp on the part of themself that has been spiraling and trapping them and hurting people around them) than letting go of an externally-sourced lie that can glom onto another character if it isn't separated refuted and destroyed \u2014 the description of Nightscapes sporadically makes statements to the effect that the Prisoner is important to the Nightscape, and this is fitting to both that &quot;emphasize the human contributions to the Darkness if its inability to create is being emphasized&quot; thing and that self-knowledge angle I mentioned earlier.<br /> <br /> I worry I may have rambled or lost the thread of my points in the course of getting this all down, but three hours writing is enough of a time investment that I have to cross my fingers I've been clear enough that I can answer follow-up questions. Hope this is helpful.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":".  Was missing a few words from the end of another sentence. 09-27-2023, 07:41 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-27T08:29:31-07:00","id":1523212,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1523019-princess-the-hopeful-crystal-edition?p=1523197#post1523197\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Other gamelines give us tyrants and conspiracies and primeval hungers made manifest making the world a worse place to secure their place in it or progress their apocalyptic plans, but Princess as presented here gives us a blind-idiot Augustinian evil that doesn't seem to even have subverted anything more unusual than bluebook Horrors, and it doesn't seem interested in examining the human contribution to that force's advancement outside the realm of caricature. I feel as though currently I cannot reasonably point to anything within the game's roster of the forces of Darkness as the tool to use for modeling the Big Bad of e.g. any given series of Pretty Cure, which seems like an inherent flaw for a game attempting to model conflict-oriented magical girl stories of any kind. (Possibly the Dark Magical Girls chapter will bring me some new information about the Twilight Queens and their Princesses that changes this impression, but six chapters in and past the section about the forces of Darkness feels like a bad place to get that turnaround).</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, I want to get to the rest of the post in full but that will take time, but I couldn't <i>possibly </i>disagree more with this.<br /> <br /> The entire <i>central thesis of the game</i> is about the human contribution to that force's advancement. It's all over everywhere. There's a supernatural corruption that gloms onto your soul and turns you evil and makes you do evil things that hurt other people. The game then says &quot;oh and that corruption, which works like the Shadowlands Taint or Generic Blizzard Fel Corruption or whatever, is something so omnipresent you don't even notice how fucked up and awful it makes you: fear.&quot; <br /> <br /> Nightscapes and Nightmares are an explicit dramatic metaphor for how people's fears manifest and hurt others. You are so afraid, you can only see the world in a certain way, and what you end up doing is just spreading that fear to others as you play out the same psychodrama in your head.<br /> <br /> The goal of how I engaged with the Darkness in this game is that it isn't a new, siloed-off thing that's distinct from what everyone experiences. That's why it's taking credit for blue-book Horrors, because when you are looking at the All-Consuming Darkness, you can't be looking at something you've never seen before. I feel like what you're looking for is stuff like &quot;vampires are evil, and they do evil stuff, but they're not responsible for anything bad because actually Humans Are The Real Monsters and saying vampires did it takes away human responsibility,&quot; which is one way to talk about &quot;human contribution to evil,&quot; but a way that always bothered me. To me, that's putting a dramatic metaphor in your story and then preventing it from BEING the thing it's a metaphor for. It's like having your magical girl develop her powers and gain a new transformation and overcome the enemy as she has a moment of emotional growth, and then saying &quot;but no this isn't actually her personal development or coming of age moment.&quot; Yes it is! That's what it's for! If it isn't, why is it in the story?<br /> <br /> The taint of the Darkness <i>isn't</i> separate from human contribution. Because you already knew that people were afraid. It's an illustration and exploration of how fear drives us to hurt others that casts people in a sympathetic light, one that focuses on how they are tormented by fear. Disquet is a metaphor for how people act when confronted with people who don't fit their expectations, but it's a NEW thing, a factor you didn't know about before you read Promethean. And that works for what Promethean is doing and what it needs to do, because its characters are as lowly as it gets, more outsider than outsider, and it doesn't matter who you are or what you do this is always going to happen to you because the Universe itself is fucking you over -- but it's also something that you caused on them, not really their fault. The Darkness can't work like that. To be a game about saving the world from The Evils, The Evils can't be a new thing. It has to be a unifying thread to The Evils existing in the world, or else what you're doing isn't fighting the Darkness of the world, you're basically just a really specialized Hunter. <br /> <br /> The taint and power of the Darkness doesn't absolve human beings or separate their actions from &quot;normal&quot; because you already knew fear was the human condition. I can't see how you don't think there's a way to do magical girl villains when that's literally what Defilers are. They are people who saw they could get dark power from spreading fear and hurting others, and went &quot;yes, I want to do that.&quot; That on its own is pure 100% human contribution even by the stricter standard. They explicitly command Darkspawn, and set up in Nightscapes and take control of them, because the thing they are a dramatic metaphor for is preying on and exploiting and stoking the fear of others to their own ends. The intro fiction, right off the bat, sets up the question of if the Nightscape was someone's <i>fault</i> and if it even matters. One of the most common conditions to create a Nightscape is guilt over your own actions. Everything front-to-back is about how humans contribute to the force's advancement. <br /> <br /> Like, I don't want to come off as yelling at you or anything, but things like this make me think we're just approaching from such fundamentally different viewpoints that there's not much purpose in trying to reconcile them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28206-huitzil37","name":"Huitzil37","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-27T16:34:42-07:00","id":1523239,"title":"","content":"I\u2019m still parsing the document, but it seems that the All-Consuming Darkness as presented isn\u2019t much different from the Dark Mother and/or the fiends of the Inferno? Or was this intentional?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-27T21:45:45-07:00","id":1523254,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1523019-princess-the-hopeful-crystal-edition?p=1523239#post1523239\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I\u2019m still parsing the document, but it seems that the All-Consuming Darkness as presented isn\u2019t much different from the Dark Mother and/or the fiends of the Inferno? Or was this intentional?</div> </div> </div> </div>The original Darkness was developed before Beast was produced. I dunno what the relationship of the writers for the First Edition was to Inferno, but I may wager they might not have read it.<br /> <br /> A smart read and write of the line between the Darkness and the Dark Mother would probably cut the line between the Dark Mother's existence as a symbol versus the Darkness having been a Pandora's Box for the various metaphysics at work in the world of Chronicles, ie, leans into it's (meta)physicality, and also draws the line that Princess originally had as a conflict point for the other monsters as effectively being creatures caught between in the shallows of the Darkness but still clawing towards the Light, particularly since the Dark Mother and her extended Families have as much a predilection to use their natures of living fear and hunger as a means to help people work out through their own darkness into a wholer actuality as they are to tear them down with it, where as the Darkness <i>only</i> has manifest interest in using a person's and people's darkness to tear them down even more.<br /> <br /> Given what Satchel has said on the subject of Nightscapes, though, I wouldn't be keen to anticipate such. And of course, I have no ready way to explain the possible relationships between Infernal Demons and the Darkness.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 09-28-2023, 12:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-27T21:58:23-07:00","id":1523255,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Huitzil37</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1523019-princess-the-hopeful-crystal-edition?p=1523212#post1523212\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay, I want to get to the rest of the post in full but that will take time, but I couldn't <i>possibly </i>disagree more with this.<br /> <br /> The entire <i>central thesis of the game</i> is about the human contribution to that force's advancement.</div> </div> </div> </div>And I am telling you that the execution I have read so far has been bad at doing that in a compelling fashion.<br /> <br /> I read the writeup on Defilers and I do not get the impression that the characters you have, again, opted to exemplify with petty criminals and monologuing warlocks are what the game wants me to use to model the villains Dark Generals work for. I read the writeup on Darkspawn in combination with the repeated statements that the Darkness doesn't have thoughts and the Cataphractoi are echoes of people running down into self-parody and I am not filled with confidence that these things will naturally lend themselves to story arcs where a semi-consistent set of antagonists work toward a plan at a scope larger than street-level menacings and private hells.<br /> <br /> The forces of Darkness not directly concerned with Nightscapes do not incline me to worry about their ability to effect large-scale operations analogous to those one might find any given antagonistic supernatural force with an agenda running, because you have spent a great deal of wordcount in the book so far telling me that the Darkness doesn't have a plan and its adherents' ambitions are base and inglorious.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's all over everywhere. There's a supernatural corruption that gloms onto your soul and turns you evil and makes you do evil things that hurt other people. The game then says &quot;oh and that corruption, which works like the Shadowlands Taint or Generic Blizzard Fel Corruption or whatever, is something so omnipresent you don't even notice how fucked up and awful it makes you: fear.&quot;<br /> <br /> Nightscapes and Nightmares are an explicit dramatic metaphor for how people's fears manifest and hurt others. You are so afraid, you can only see the world in a certain way, and what you end up doing is just spreading that fear to others as you play out the same psychodrama in your head. </div> </div> </div>They also exist independently of people and explicitly will still do so even if you manage to convince the Prisoners at their core they can leave, whereupon the Nightscape can grab onto someone else, which 1) kind of strips away the personal angle to the psychodrama and 2) does not convince me Nightmares should be modeled differently from spirits.<br /> <br /> An interesting thing Werewolf 1e said way back in its corebook that hasn't been as explicit about since is that narratively spirits represent <i>consequences</i>, up to and including Wounds just being the spiritual reflection of sites of atrocity, because the Shadow is where the undying reflection of what goes on in the world lives and propagates itself, such that getting rid of a spirit problem almost always involves addressing the underlying issue in addition to thrashing a giant six-headed metaphor to cut the problem out root and branch. It feels nicely into the other part of that book, where it points out that flipping out and killing shit is going to usually produce a less desirable outcome than social engineering.<br /> <br /> Chronicles already has a metaphysical corner of the universe where direct corruptive evil lives in the form of the Inferno, and it generally makes its bones on the exploitation of Vices and other weaknesses, such that there's chapter fiction in its book inverting the parable about only one set of footprints in the sand. Twisted ghosts and spirits and ideaforms give way to raw demonic power that can then fade to whispers in susceptible ears subtle enough to pass for intrusive thoughts, and one of the things Inferno makes explicit is that, unlike spirits (who are the sum of their parts), its demons have free will, and exercise that will to cause harm and spread corruption and despoil the world. It runs on evil and subverted human imagination, and given the way one of its main writers has described his perspective on the subject I would contend that it's a good example of Augustinian evil done in a compelling fashion.<br /> <br /> The Darkness can have overlap with these and other supernatural elements, but at a certain point it becomes necessary to actually draw lines with regards to what it actually is on a Watsonian level. Saying that The Darkness Is Fear, By Which We Mean This Particular Extreme Of Fear, But Also Bereft Of Any Connection To Any Other Supernatural Force, But Also It's The Source Of All Other Supernatural Beings Too is a particularly knotty thread to weave into a Chronicles of Darkness game without ignoring a lot of things about the Chronicles setting, which is one of the main reasons I say that the game currently feels more like it's telling you it's set in the Chronicles of Darkness rather than actually meshing the genre with the setting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The goal of how I engaged with the Darkness in this game is that it isn't a new, siloed-off thing that's distinct from what everyone experiences. That's why it's taking credit for blue-book Horrors, because when you are looking at the All-Consuming Darkness, you can't be looking at something you've never seen before. I feel like what you're looking for is stuff like &quot;vampires are evil, and they do evil stuff, but they're not responsible for anything bad because actually Humans Are The Real Monsters and saying vampires did it takes away human responsibility,&quot; which is one way to talk about &quot;human contribution to evil,&quot; but a way that always bothered me. </div> </div> </div> That feeling you are having about what I am looking for is mistaken; see the above vis-a-vis &quot;If you are going to say the Darkness can't make any grand plans on its own, it behooves you to not paint the antagonists aligned with it as agents bereft of any higher goals or ability to plan in the long term.&quot;<br /> <br /> If your intention is to claim that magical girl stories are a natural fit for the setting, the Storyteller's toolbox for Princess should implicitly if not explicitly facilitate the structure of a magical girl story, whether that means clarifying that Nightscapes are the heart of a season-long arc that characters close in on or work their way through, allowing major antagonists to increase in power and threat as the protagonists develop, or tidying up the language treating the Darkness itself as a moral actor rather than a power source so it doesn't distract from the human elements who are actually steering the boat.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The taint of the Darkness <i>isn't</i> separate from human contribution. Because you already knew that people were afraid. It's an illustration and exploration of how fear drives us to hurt others that casts people in a sympathetic light, one that focuses on how they are tormented by fear. Disquet is a metaphor for how people act when confronted with people who don't fit their expectations, but it's a NEW thing, a factor you didn't know about before you read Promethean. And that works for what Promethean is doing and what it needs to do, because its characters are as lowly as it gets, more outsider than outsider, and it doesn't matter who you are or what you do this is always going to happen to you because the Universe itself is fucking you over -- but it's also something that you caused on them, not really their fault. The Darkness can't work like that. To be a game about saving the world from The Evils, The Evils can't be a new thing. It has to be a unifying thread to The Evils existing in the world, or else what you're doing isn't fighting the Darkness of the world, you're basically just a really specialized Hunter. </div> </div> </div>And part of where your presentation is running aground of itself is the repeated attempts within the text to silo off the Darkness from any connection to the established supernatural elements of the larger setting when devs and writers have pointed out that in the grand scheme of things the supernatural is not apart from the world, which is what I'm getting at when I say that the world is weird \u2014 saying that Nightmares don't generate Resonance and Mage Sight can't tell anything about the Darkness and whatnot comes across as incoherent when Resonance is &quot;there is e.g. a dog/a car/death/sadness here,&quot; Mage Sight can provide completely mundane information about the subjects of its observation, and human-accessible ritual magic largely operates by using symbolism to take advantage of obscure ways the world works.<br /> <br /> Speaking of the way the world works, the inclination of the text toward unsympathetic caricature does not mesh well with the generally sympathetic reaction to &quot;people are afraid&quot; and the specific antagonist rationale of &quot;I'm making other people afraid because I am afraid,&quot; especially when you're making a game dealing with a genre for which the Power of Love, Friendship, Kindness, Understanding, etc is so front and center. The Evils can't be a new thing, yes, but you also can't then paint the people The Evils are carried out through as uniformly irredeemable while also denying The Evils any sort of meaningful agency.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The taint and power of the Darkness doesn't absolve human beings or separate their actions from &quot;normal&quot; because you already knew fear was the human condition. I can't see how you don't think there's a way to do magical girl villains when that's literally what Defilers are. They are people who saw they could get dark power from spreading fear and hurting others, and went &quot;yes, I want to do that.&quot; That on its own is pure 100% human contribution even by the stricter standard. They explicitly command Darkspawn, and set up in Nightscapes and take control of them, because the thing they are a dramatic metaphor for is preying on and exploiting and stoking the fear of others to their own ends. The intro fiction, right off the bat, sets up the question of if the Nightscape was someone's <i>fault</i> and if it even matters. One of the most common conditions to create a Nightscape is guilt over your own actions. Everything front-to-back is about how humans contribute to the force's advancement. </div> </div> </div>And everything front-to-back that you have presented about the Darkness has made it clear that it is an idiot that makes idiots and turns you into an idiot if you serve it, which suggests that if the intention is for Defilers to be the brains of the operation who are the main ones running the show then they ought to have more attention dedicated to them in the antagonist writeups, because presently they have a short writeup at the end of the list of the forces of Darkness and a pair of low-rung example characters, behind a menagerie of monsters with some degree of severe dysfunctionality.<br /> \u200b<br /> My contention is about the lack of examination of the human contributions to the Darkness's advancement <i>outside the realm of caricature</i>, alongside the Darkness's lack of presented threats that scale beyond the scope of a neighborhood or maybe a city due to the limitations of its agents and the forwarded pettiness of those who exploit it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-28T07:44:29-07:00","id":1523273,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1523019-princess-the-hopeful-crystal-edition?p=1523255#post1523255\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The forces of Darkness not directly concerned with Nightscapes do not incline me to worry about their ability to effect large-scale operations analogous to those one might find any given antagonistic supernatural force with an agenda running, because you have spent a great deal of wordcount in the book so far telling me that the Darkness doesn't have a plan and its adherents' ambitions are base and inglorious.</div> </div> </div> </div>So your objection is not to the concept, it's to the fact that the sample enemies presented are low-level? That is something I can change. I figured you were supposed to lean towards lower-level threats as samples in the core book, since players are going to encounter a lot more of those than the higher level ones, and the real high-level boss monsters are for Night Horrors. More detail on the Defilers and Cataphractoi is definitely doable. I was worried about too much detail, because D/V definitely had that problem, but if I went too far in the other direction that's something to note and fix. I just don't get why you're so convinced this is a problem of the whole basis of the antagonistic force being deficient, and not &quot;the examples aren't good enough.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If your intention is to claim that magical girl stories are a natural fit for the setting, the Storyteller's toolbox for Princess should implicitly if not explicitly facilitate the structure of a magical girl story, whether that means clarifying that Nightscapes are the heart of a season-long arc that characters close in on or work their way through, allowing major antagonists to increase in power and threat as the protagonists develop, or tidying up the language treating the Darkness itself as a moral actor rather than a power source so it doesn't distract from the human elements who are actually steering the boat. </div> </div> </div> I say... all of those things? Why would you assume that antagonists can't grow in power? Or that people don't use it as a power source when I describe it multiple times as exactly that? Is this really, seriously, entirely because the two sample Defilers weren't big enough? Why would you say that the rules for Nightscapes being a major endeavor to close in on or work their way through aren't the heart of a season-long arc about closing in on or working their way through?<br /> <br /> I'm going to be honest here, the criticisms you're giving on the thematic basis are baffling, they're just so diametrically opposed from what pretty much every other person in every other venue has said, whether they're my friend or not. (Then seeing ArcaneArts being convinced it's going to be bad and telling other people it's going to be bad without having read it is... not a great thing to see.) While I'm obviously biased because I made it, I'm not sure if <i>anything</i> could stand up to a reading this hostile. &quot;Evil can't create and can only twist and destroy, so it's ultimately pathetic, not grandiose&quot; is a really, really common motif in a lot of fiction, and it doesn't make the evil less threatening to people. The Darkness is a blind mad idiot and it makes things that have no aspiration above hurting people and the people who embrace it become worse at everything other than hurting people but they keep doing it and and keep spreading misery and destruction and it's fundamentally wrong and unjust that this happens so much and that people have to fall down this path to protect themselves from everyone else who fell down this path. It does make you a worse person and it does diminish you but it doesn't make you less of a threat to people. This is not Fallout 3 and evil is not a perfectly symmetrical equal to good; it gives you power but not the power to do things that matter, and that's... that's how a whole shitload of evil forces in fiction work, this isn't new. This is how the Dark Side of the Force works, this is how the Shadowlands Taint works, hell, it's how viruses in Resident Evil work (everyone's talking about how it gives them so much power but it's always only the power to be a goo monster). I'm not shy or secretive about this being how it works here.<br /> <br /> I don't focus on larger grandiose schemes because that's not really what the CofD's focus is. it's about local things and local play, but then you say this makes it impossible to go bigger? There's really obvious hooks for larger scale things (there's the whole &quot;three people who betrayed humanity who are still out there with some sinister plots we don't know&quot; and &quot;sentient torture-dimensions looming on the horizon like Walpurgisnacht&quot; and &quot;extradimensional city of Dark Magical Girls harvesting hope and spreading despair in worldwide operations&quot; and &quot;fantasy isekai kingdoms that are explicitly where you can do big sweeping adventures that would otherwise change the setting too much to be recognizable,&quot;) but I spend most of the time talking about the local threats because that's probably where the game is going to be happening. Vampires deal with local politics. Werewolf states there's untouchable Rank 6+ spirit-gods but then talks about the local threats you face and local problems you have to deal with. Changeling has the True Fae, a threat which you're never going to beat and your efforts are focused on avoiding and mostly serve to inform the interactions you have with the Changelings and Court dealings around you. To be fair, Mage has more attention to the Grand Scope Of Things and Geist talks in some detail about the entire unjust system of the Underworld you are implied to have to deal with, but I think if you turned the same criticism on most of the games in the CofD, they'd fail. Even in Demon the God-Machine's plans at large are not a thing you thwart or really interact with, it's just what's relevant to you; the God-Machine is more of a medium through which things happen than an agent unto itself and that's pretty much exactly what the Darkness is. The spirit world is not agentic itself (I mean, as I read it all spirits are pretty much compulsive in their behavior, can't do anything else, like they can't even form mens rea) but causes things that you have to deal with by mechanisms you know, it's like an occult law of physics, which is also how the Darkness works. The spirit world is allowed to represent consequences, but the Darkness isn't allowed to represent the destructive and self-propagating nature of fear? The central street-level problem is literally people being tortured by their own fears as those fears go out and terrorize others, and those people need to be rescued and understood and convinced to let go of their terror and accept that they don't have to live like this, but the game says that they're irrdeemable? Which I... never do even about Defilers and the only irredeemable ones are monsters of the week that aren't human?<br /> <br /> Like you keep bringing back &quot;Nightmares and Nightscapes can exist after the Prisoner leaves&quot; as if that invalidates all symbolism and metaphor about them, instead of being &quot;one person's fears can have lasting consequences that they've inflicted on others even after they think they're over it.&quot; Like how someone's weird conspiracy theory can take on a life of its own and convince and even entrap others in it. I already figured I was spelling out a LOT more of the dramatic metaphors than most other games do, and when you say it doesn't do that I don't know if there's something wrong with me or something wrong with you.<br /> <br /> Like I said. I feel like our perspectives are so completely and diametrically different we're practically speaking different languages. You put forth criticisms to which I can only say &quot;I already did that, though?&quot; I'm already worried about repeating myself too much, I expected people to point out redundancies in them. I just, seriously, I think this might be an unbridgeable gap, and if I made something that fit your criteria it would fail a lot more people's criteria. Sincerely, I do thank you for the feedback, and you've obviously put a lot of effort into it, and I think the most actionable piece is &quot;beef up the Defilers&quot; and I'll do that and hope it helps, but I think we're just across an incredibly wide inferential gap here. I'm getting more frustrated, obviously, and I would imagine you are too, and I don't want to just devolve into yelling at each other.<br /> <br /> I'd really, really like it if ArcaneArts wasn't telling people to expect it to be bad or shallow without reading it, though. That isn't nice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28206-huitzil37","name":"Huitzil37","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-07T18:14:41-07:00","id":1523803,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Huitzil37</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1523019-princess-the-hopeful-crystal-edition?p=1523273#post1523273\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So your objection is not to the concept, it's to the fact that the sample enemies presented are low-level? That is something I can change. I figured you were supposed to lean towards lower-level threats as samples in the core book, since players are going to encounter a lot more of those than the higher level ones, and the real high-level boss monsters are for Night Horrors.</div> </div> </div> </div>Generally speaking best-practice is that if an antagonist type is meant to span a wide range of difficulty levels the examples of that antagonist type should include at least one sample character not purely reducible to a generic description. Werewolf 2e provides as example spirits both a fear-Gaffling and a powerful spirit-noble of the electrical grid. Demon gives example angels that include a Rank 1 serial killer, a Rank 3 demon-tempter, and a Rank 5 hunter angel. Changeling 2e provides example hobgoblins ranging from a nine-year-old Goblin Queen to a dispossessed homeowner to a sentient and hungry cave system. Your own Darkspawn writeups, however mechanically unfinished some of them currently are, specifically include a heavy-hitter along with the Brief Nightmares.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I just don't get why you're so convinced this is a problem of the whole basis of the antagonistic force being deficient, and not &quot;the examples aren't good enough.&quot; </div> </div> </div>Because the examples being low-level is in line with the rest of what you spend the entire book telling the reader about the antagonistic force, including focusing on the handicaps of the things serving that force over the actual threats they can pose and putting the characters aligned with that force who are not as handicapped by it in the backseat (if not the trunk) of the vehicle they should theoretically be driving.<br /> <br /> The examples aren't good enough <i>and</i> the book doesn't steer the reader toward making better examples.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why would you assume that antagonists can't grow in power? </div> </div> </div>I have little reason to assume the antagonists <i>are interested in</i> growing in power when you have presented mechanics and narratives that:<br /> <br /> 1. Stop the cannon-fodder you-can-kill-these-without-remorse grunts from getting stronger without individually being actively prevented from doing the thing they exist to do, at which point they are forced to become more complex and develop higher thought that they resent using \u2014 since Darkspawn only form and act in response to the presence of the Light or direction from Command Darkspawn and the like, unless their evolution is intended to be <i>extremely</i> rapid, having the baseline threat level of the Darkspawn in a given arc or area go up without invoking fiat means such things need to be <i>prepared</i> since they're not immortal by default and there is no good non-situational reason for the protagonists to leave them alive.<br /> <br /> 2. Put the direct agents of Darkness that can do human things on a timer while also making it clear that even their ability to pass for human is limited \u2014 they may have Aspirations (unless they're the reason some of the Fractured Conditions reference Urges, which does not help the case against the point I am illustrating), but they're more and more exaggerated toward their Dread and Drive the stronger they are and roughly a third of their writeup is dedicated to hammering the notion into the reader that these are empty creatures of Darkness who can only act like people for a short while, which in turn steers the mind away from alternatives to the &quot;gleefully spread fear because they want to&quot; and &quot;unknowingly do nothing but spread fear&quot; models described early on. Defilers can become these things accidentally or on purpose and that frankly doesn't look like a much better outcome than the &quot;bully or evil warlock&quot; presentation their own writeup tilts toward when volunteer Cataphractoi are painted as suckers who didn't realize they'd be killed and replaced with a degenerating mental double. We're given a rough idea of how Darkspawn advance in power and for Cataphractoi we're instead given a vague notion that they will eventually stop being rational threats whether they get stronger or not. They're a danger with an expiration date and a funky smell already coming from the carton.<br /> <br /> 3. Paint the Darkness itself as a cosmic force with no thoughts that we nonetheless need to be told repeatedly is petty and awful and whose purest manifestations have the problems described above, and therefore has no intentions toward anything other than continuing to try to terrify people, which is better managed with Nightmares dependent upon the formation of a Nightscape than making stronger Darkspawn.<br /> <br /> As-presented the Forces of Darkness read like antagonists from Promethean that have been transplanted into conflict with a werewolf pack, except the Pandorans don't grow from stealing Pyros and can only kind of become sublimati, the rare natural-born sublimati's intelligence is undercut by more than their hunger for Pyros, and the alchemists aren't dealing with a morally neutral substance.<br /> <br /> It also feels really weird to put forth Devil May Cry as emblematic of what you want out of this game and implicitly compare the Darkness with the demonic powers of that game when the main character is the son of a devil who <i>woke up to justice, fell in love, and started a family</i>. There's more than one magical girl show whose endgame heroic roster includes characters who were nonhuman villains who had a change of heart and/or changed sides because of a desire to become stronger by emulating the people they keep losing to, and Princess currently doesn't readily allow that to happen because the monsters have to be Made Of Evil and therefore incapable of positive growth.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Or that people don't use it as a power source when I describe it multiple times as exactly that? </div> </div> </div>As previously with &quot;outside the realm of caricature,&quot; you are ignoring the very important other half of the sentence: <i>treating the Darkness as a moral actor</i>. If the Darkness itself is not important as a character then the reader does not need a solid quarter of its wordcount dedicated to telling you that fire burns and mold makes you sick instead of elucidating the crimes of the arsonists and mold-monsters making people afraid to go near historic buildings. It can be an unnatural disaster or it can be the kernel of an intelligent bastard's master plan, but if the forces of Darkness are supposed to be a focal antagonist then you cannot replicate the recurring problem with zombie apocalypse fiction and still expect people to focus on the zombies as long-term adversaries worth extended screentime. It becomes just so much background noise.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Why would you say that the rules for Nightscapes being a major endeavor to close in on or work their way through aren't the heart of a season-long arc about closing in on or working their way through? </div> </div> </div>Because the presentation of Nightscapes doesn't actually pin down the scale and frequency the game expects you to use them with.<br /> <br /> Nightscapes can be about fears so minor it's a shock to learn what they are. Their psychodrama can amount to a single small tableau. The tipping point for their formation is functionally at the same level of diabolical intervention as the attention of a Watchtower kicking off an Awakening, except the kind of people who enter a Mystery Play is narrower than &quot;get a particular result on a breaking point and meet another criterion that is not explained further.&quot; They supposedly abrade the border separating the real world from the Dark World and are affected to some degree by Dark activity in the Astral Pools, but no guidance is given on how they interact besides that some Nightscapes can only be reached through the Dark World and Bleeds can be formed by their getting bigger or smaller (when their Potency is described as representing their power and scale, only loosely correlates to the strength of its emotional core and effect as adjusted by more unspecified mysterious criteria, and doesn't actually describe if or how they can expand).<br /> <br /> If Nightscapes (or even a notable subset of them) are supposed to be long-term problems reserved for climactic points in the narrative <i>rather than</i> the full scope from Victim Of The Week to Final Boss Dungeon, it does not help to have so many undeclared variables skewing the scope of their impact <i>downward</i>. The thinly-veiled reference to DnD in the Creating Nightscapes section elides the fact that high-fantasy adventure dungeons cover a <i>wide</i> spread from &quot;two-floor basement with a revenant at the bottom&quot; to &quot;basically Castlevania&quot; and one of those takes the equivalent of an episode or two of TV while the other is an endgame arc unto itself.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;Evil can't create and can only twist and destroy, so it's ultimately pathetic, not grandiose&quot; is a really, really common motif in a lot of fiction, and it doesn't make the evil less threatening to people. The Darkness is a blind mad idiot and it makes things that have no aspiration above hurting people and the people who embrace it become worse at everything other than hurting people but they keep doing it and and keep spreading misery and destruction and it's fundamentally wrong and unjust that this happens so much and that people have to fall down this path to protect themselves from everyone else who fell down this path. It does make you a worse person and it does diminish you but it doesn't make you less of a threat to people. This is not Fallout 3 and evil is not a perfectly symmetrical equal to good; it gives you power but not the power to do things that matter, and that's... that's how a whole shitload of evil forces in fiction work, this isn't new. This is how the Dark Side of the Force works, this is how the Shadowlands Taint works, hell, it's how viruses in Resident Evil work (everyone's talking about how it gives them so much power but it's always only the power to be a goo monster). I'm not shy or secretive about this being how it works here. </div> </div> </div>The cosmic and local pettiness of the Darkness and its forces as you've written them, however, makes it an uphill slope trying to justify antagonists who are trying to do anything more ambitiously evil than recurring street-level felonies and acts of terrorism. It feels firmly pinned to the monster-of-the-week part of the genre and seems to actively sabotage the kind of semi-unified front for which multiple &quot;Dark Generals&quot; might collaborate in a magical girl story without the Cataphractoi's issues.<br /> <br /> When the final villain of the series shows up in the endgame of a story and shows how close they are to Pure Darkness by being devoid of empathy, compassion, and virtue, it is a proper shock because it has been out of focus while its more relatable executives have been the face of the opposition and eventually get resorbed for failing too many times. That doesn't work if all the links in the chain of command are equally or minimally differently abominable and the camera has as much contempt for the personlike actors as the insta-goons they control and the cosmic background radiation that empowers them.<br /> <br /> The writing about the forces of Darkness is currently fixated on either jumping to the top of the ladder of evil or never leaving its first rung, and that limits the things the Noble can be expected to have to overcome just as much as if Batman's antagonist roster never moved beyond Joe Chill and other street-level criminals.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't focus on larger grandiose schemes because that's not really what the CofD's focus is. it's about local things and local play, but then you say this makes it impossible to go bigger? There's really obvious hooks for larger scale things (there's the whole &quot;three people who betrayed humanity who are still out there with some sinister plots we don't know&quot; </div> </div> </div>The Dark Queens are mentioned in the backstory of the Kingdom's fall and the enlistment of the Wardens and not much else is done with them besides; I find the Four Heavenly Kings hooks similarly thin. A prelapsarian betrayal and an undetailed period of adventures by characters whose other activities are entirely blank with no suggestions for how these can meaningfully affect the local play are not terribly useful story hooks.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;sentient torture-dimensions looming on the horizon like Walpurgisnacht&quot; </div> </div> </div>The Enthroned have the same issue of caricature as the Cataphractoi and the same issue of intended scale as Nightscapes. Walpurgisnacht was a typhoon both literally and metaphorically, and most Witches were not Walpurgisnacht.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;extradimensional city of Dark Magical Girls harvesting hope and spreading despair in worldwide operations&quot; </div> </div> </div>As presented, Alhambra's agents are operating at the same scale as PCs are, mostly work alone, are nominally meant to be sympathetic characters in specific contrast to the nonhumans you are expected to kill, and belong to an apparatus invested in the PCs continuing to be alive and unexposed to such a degree that their home base is considered neutral ground for the protagonists.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;fantasy isekai kingdoms that are explicitly where you can do big sweeping adventures that would otherwise change the setting too much to be recognizable,&quot; </div> </div> </div>I don't know what you think &quot;You can effect sweeping changes in a part of the setting the book explicitly tells you matters dramatically less than the real world&quot; does for a stated problem with scalable plot-scope, but it's not much.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Vampires deal with local politics. Werewolf states there's untouchable Rank 6+ spirit-gods but then talks about the local threats you face and local problems you have to deal with. Changeling has the True Fae, a threat which you're never going to beat and your efforts are focused on avoiding and mostly serve to inform the interactions you have with the Changelings and Court dealings around you. To be fair, Mage has more attention to the Grand Scope Of Things and Geist talks in some detail about the entire unjust system of the Underworld you are implied to have to deal with, but I think if you turned the same criticism on most of the games in the CofD, they'd fail. Even in Demon the God-Machine's plans at large are not a thing you thwart or really interact with, it's just what's relevant to you; the God-Machine is more of a medium through which things happen than an agent unto itself and that's pretty much exactly what the Darkness is. The spirit world is not agentic itself (I mean, as I read it all spirits are pretty much compulsive in their behavior, can't do anything else, like they can't even form mens rea) but causes things that you have to deal with by mechanisms you know, it's like an occult law of physics, which is also how the Darkness works. </div> </div> </div>Vampire's local politics includes setting up a biopolitical machine that lets the Kindred of an area feed securely and better fill the niche they've carved out, Werewolf attaches those Rank 6+ spirits to those local threats and problems through the way the Shadow and its &quot;society&quot; work, Changeling has the True Fae's ambition to possess the entire world serve as the most straightforward explanation for why the Hedge butts into the worlds of dreams/mirrors/cyberspace (and those Court dealings include upholding obligations to powerful beings and forces), and the God-Machine's general inscrutability doesn't change the fact that it notionally has <i>plans</i>. To bring Deviant in as another point of comparison, the transformed <i>work to dismantle conspiracies that turn people into monsters in the service of Principles</i>, which gives the game a functional sense of potential scope even though the driving goal for most of the Remade is &quot;you really wanna kill that guy.&quot;<br /> <br /> Princess: Crystal Edition currently puts the Darkness forward as the primary source of antagonists, tells the reader it's got a connection to the origins of the wider supernatural that it nevertheless does not link up with beyond vague gestures toward declaring a bluebook Horror is one of its servants, paints its direct manifestations as minimally functional at best and cannon fodder at worst, puts its agents either so far in the background that they're only mentioned in the mythic history and a couple of extremely broad rumors or so far to the ground level that their ability to subvert a pocket torture-dimension is just part of the generally-stated capacity for those things to be subverted, and otherwise shows little interest in examples of what its forces might be trying to accomplish in any given city and why beyond the level of detail of &quot;the Darkness is evil and it wants you to be scared out of your mind.&quot;<br /> <br /> The monsters are obvious when the fact that specific ones can be capable of subterfuge isn't the point of those specimens, the agents with plans that could be derailed are out of focus, and unless you want to try and wrestle with the problem of making a splat modeling a classically reactive-heroic genre be proactive without smacking into a &quot;focus on virtue and public service&quot; that you've assessed as a problem with previous versions of the game you are going to have to put more in the book that supports Princesses making a major victory than &quot;we got rid of a Dark manifestation with a big number on it and thereby saved its victims from supernaturally-manifested terror.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The spirit world is allowed to represent consequences, but the Darkness isn't allowed to represent the destructive and self-propagating nature of fear? </div> </div> </div>The spirit world <i>gives consequences that can scale</i> by the nature of what those consequences are. The things the book tells us about the Darkness can be true, but the emphasis placed on different parts of that body of information affects how it is received. If the Darkness is not a meaningful character in itself I do not need to be told that it is a &quot;petty, short-sighted, venal, cruel, <i>disgusting</i> little bastard&quot; when the more pressing issue is the Defiler industrialist using its power to seed Nightscapes to facilitate paving over an underprivileged neighborhood, or the Cataphract in the guise of a demagogue raising hell about how Those People want to make your children just like them, or the poor mistaken pair (one soulless, one having read the wrong literature) who think if they can just feed enough people to the Dark World in the right way they can carve a hole to a Paradise free from fear.<br /> <br /> The pure-evil grunts can get away with having their interiority out of focus, as can the raw force that births them, but the point of having a simplistic force of corruption infect more complex characters is to allow those other characters to be complex and focal characters in the narrative, <i>who can then demonstrate</i> the destructive and self-propagating nature of fear in their makeup. As it stands the only antagonists the game explicitly wants you to be sympathetic to are the Twilight Princesses, and even then the ones who share Fractured Conditions with Cataphractoi as a mechanical widget shoulder most of the contempt.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The central street-level problem is literally people being tortured by their own fears as those fears go out and terrorize others, and those people need to be rescued and understood and convinced to let go of their terror and accept that they don't have to live like this, but the game says that they're irrdeemable? Which I... never do even about Defilers and the only irredeemable ones are monsters of the week that aren't human? </div> </div> </div>Setting aside the Nightscape scaling problem I've already addressed and setting aside the worrying language of &quot;if a monster isn't human it's okay to kill it,&quot; if you're saying I'm not supposed to read things like &quot;knowingly using the power of the Darkness crosses the line between being an asshole and being an evil warlock&quot; and &quot;the global operation that steals hope and siphons despair, whose field agents are explicitly people you are expected to mutually not want to kill despite their also temporarily using Darkspawn, is very insistent on drawing a line between what they do and what these people do&quot; and &quot;there is something wrong with these people,&quot; as &quot;these people are irredeemable&quot; then I do not know what kind of picture you were trying to paint.<br /> <br /> My statement about Scared People Scaring People was referring to Defilers, in the same sense that <i>Dreams of Avarice</i> highlights how unjust systems punish the people in power for trying to be compassionate or relinquish their power as much as they encourage cruelty and the jealous hoarding of their power. I do not have any trouble seeing the game's sympathy for the people it calls &quot;Prisoners,&quot; who serves as the cores of self-perpetuating Torment Vortices that spill out into the world around them, but the problem I am expressing is that sympathy for antagonists is thin on the ground and Prisoners are not consistently antagonists.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Like you keep bringing back &quot;Nightmares and Nightscapes can exist after the Prisoner leaves&quot; as if that invalidates all symbolism and metaphor about them, instead of being &quot;one person's fears can have lasting consequences that they've inflicted on others even after they think they're over it.&quot; Like how someone's weird conspiracy theory can take on a life of its own and convince and even entrap others in it. </div> </div> </div>If the Nightscape systems had any interaction with the Dreamlands/Astral Pools systems besides the statement that Darkness-tainted locations are more prone to <i>forming</i> Nightscapes, this would be markedly more resonant with the text. As it stands, the persistence of a freestanding uninhabited Nightscape following the departure of the Prisoner doesn't do this well without contrivance \u2014 destroying a Nightscape's Corpus and letting loose a bunch of terrifying Rogue Nightmares is bound to roll the dice a bunch on potentially causing new Nightscapes to form, but 1) filling the Psyche track stops the Nightscape's compulsion to enact its psychodrama and there is no indication that Destroying an Abandoned Nightscape gives its Nightmares a renewed compulsion, and 2) see the above bit about lack of guidance re: how big and commonplace Nightscapes are supposed to be.<br /> <br /> <br /> I finished the book a week ago, so running through my remaining thoughts:<br /> <br /> It would be good to get a little more to go off of for the Twilight Queens besides Tears ostensibly thinking the Radiant Queens are and should be their allies on the practical end of things, considering the presented hangups of Storms and Mirrors, and the notion of alliance &quot;if the situation is dire enough&quot; is, I think, another thing that warrants better definition of the higher-level threats posed by the forces of Darkness unless there's some completely separate axis of threat that's supposed to prompt that sort of teamwork.<br /> <br /> &quot;Breaking points caused by sorrow and despair&quot; feel ill-defined for Alhambra's power source in a game where most breaking points come from fear or pain or compromise.<br /> <br /> If Alhambran ghosts work so differently from regular ghosts and Ravens do not have any sort of innate abilities relating to regular ghosts, it may be prudent not to equate the two unless being removed from Alhambra is possible and has some effect worth mentioning.<br /> <br /> The notion of the Kingdom Come feels conceptually anemic for the same reason as the story hooks around the Dark Queens and the Four Heavenly Kings fall flat \u2014 it's presently a pie-in-the-sky aspiration without much in the way of examples of what people have tried for it besides talking over the internet, supposedly if it succeeded the Noble would be collectively vulnerable if it succeeded and so Alhambra actively sabotages it despite their being as close as currently exists to a global organization of magical girls, and nothing less-monolithic like trying to assemble a cellular cooperative structure at a scope approaching Alhambra's is floated. It feels like if Demon brought up Hell three times in the course of the book and never talked about what Tempters and Saboteurs and Inquisitors did to pursue it.<br /> <br /> Nomenclature problem: I recognize and can infer broadly what an &quot;Echo&quot; is from the handful of times it shows up in the book, but it is never actually defined in the Lexicon nor given even loose mechanical effects outside of the Beacon Merit.<br /> <br /> The interaction between the Black Lightning quote from the Storms writeup and the game's take on the Darkness as Overwhelming Fear does less than useful things for both.<br /> <br /> The writeup on Mirrors does not convince me of the Inspirational Media section's assertion that Vergil Son of Sparda is a perfect example of a Prince of Mirrors, particularly in a post-DMCV world, because the writeup is chiefly focused on Brats being delusional narcissists and Vergil's pursuit of solitary power has different explicit rationale altogether.<br /> <br /> The Molemountain writeup gives me cause to note that if you need reasons for the Dreamlands to be relevant in a given story and justify the lack of unified Noble society you could do worse than exploiting the Gale-Blind nature of the Queens and their Lucid Princesses to have a given Lucid Domain be the closest loss in the Dream Front at the start of a beginning group's story a la the various &quot;fairy fleeing the evil army's takeover with a macguffin&quot; openings to any given series of Pretty Cure, which in turn can tilt the particular manifestations of the Darkness dealt with in that first arc or set of arcs toward a given theme or motif. (This would, of course, have to go hand in hand with better integrating the Darkness's impact on the Dreamlands to its effects on Nightscapes and the waking world.)<br /> <br /> The statement that the Darkness is not as limitless as the Dreamlands combined with the statement that the Queens are nevertheless basically in a forever war with it on the Dream Front does little to help the impression that the Darkness is more of an occasionally terrifying nuisance than an existential threat under any circumstances short of the end of the world.<br /> <br /> I do not understand why the permanently Lucid need full statblocks if they exist in the numbers they are described as having.<br /> <br /> The way Transformation works in the Dreamlands does not help me understand why it doesn't simply use a Dream Form.<br /> <br /> I do not believe it is helpful to the book to directly suggest using the Dreamlands to completely abandon the main stage of the game. In an Appendix or a Shards supplement, perhaps, but the corebook is for telling players and Storytellers how the game is built to facilitate the stories you made it to tell. The Chronicles setting is built such that drastic changes can be rolled with for the purposes of the story and most people know enough about the way the world works to keep mum while there's bigger things to worry about. If the book is going to say magical girls are a natural fit for the Chronicles of Darkness it should not then backpedal and say that the best way to play out sweeping changes from the norm is in a dreamworld that doesn't matter.<br /> <br /> Attributing Beacons' increased supernatural fuel-harvesting yields to their connection to the Light is making a metaphysical statement about the Light that I don't think you intended, and the Merit's Integrity prerequisite feels odd when combined with its being a condition for becoming Sworn and the Twilight Queens have Sworn too (does it go away if you hit Integrity 5?).<br /> <br /> I can see the connective tissue of &quot;Sworn are to Princesses as Princesses are to Queens,&quot; but I'm not sure I recognize the mahou shoujo trope Sworn are meant to emulate, and for the same reason I don't know that the Charm system works for Beacons as a model of &quot;B-splat has minor powers.&quot; It may be worthwhile to get some unity of concept on the &quot;supporting cast&quot; powers instead of the current somewhat awkward fitting of PC powers to suit them.<br /> <br /> I do not know what effects the Sworn having an unraisable Inner Light of 0 is supposed to interact with, besides the very few mechanics that just differentiate mortals from &quot;anything with a Supernatural Tolerance Trait.&quot;<br /> <br /> The Shikigami section has an awful lot of the book's recurring issues with consistent tone, and its suggestions for &quot;knobs&quot; would probably be better placed in a proper Storytelling chapter. The means of incarnation feel arbitrarily untidy without a further examination of what coming out of the Dreamlands entails.<br /> <br /> Shikigami Benefits and the concept of minor shikigami are interesting, though I'm a little confused as to why Lore is associated with Restoration instead of Ward (given the other Benefits resonate with the less &quot;active&quot; Charms from the Calling affinities).<br /> <br /> It feels weird for Embassies to come with a weakness without addressing whether this is some form of compromise with the world or not, particularly when this edition of the game includes the option to permanently limit Charms' default applications; most splats that pick up additional weaknesses for power do so from a power-has-a-price perspective that seems at odds with the idealism of the Light of Hope.<br /> <br /> The Embassy to Death's signature Charm feels at odds with its stated function, as well as either warranting discussion of Alhambra's ghosts or being another mark against Alhambra's ghosts being treated the same as conventional ghosts.<br /> <br /> The Embassy to Night's Reminiscent Plea feels like it specifically makes things worse in the aftermath, which is at odds with its stated function.<br /> <br /> I know the Embassy to Oceans is a legacy inclusion, but I was honestly kind of surprised at the lack of an Embassy to the Internet in the vein of e.g. Corrector Yui, what with the recurring mentions of hacker Nobles, Alhambra seeding disinformation through social media, and the risks posed by the Enthroned knowing how to use the web.<br /> <br /> In Regalia, the Guard tag not being Melee-locked and the Firearm tag being the only way for Ranged Weapon Regalia to innately ignore Defense both feel weird.<br /> <br /> Some of the Conditions and Tilts in the Appendix corresponding to singular powers and Merits incline me to wonder why they're modeled as Conditions and Tilts at all instead of printing their effects in the one place they're actually used.<br /> <br /> Broadly I feel as though the introduction of Intensity as a systems term causes more problems than it solves as far as making references simpler when fully ninety percent of its application can just use &quot;successes.&quot;<br /> <br /> Overall I still feel that the specific emotional angle chosen for the Darkness doesn't track with the genre the game is ostensibly imitating (fear does not interface with hope in the same way despair does, and fear is usually an emotional step <i>up</i> from the deadened affect or nihilistic pursuits more commonly both more commonly seen the source media and more appropriate to forces of existential threat in Chronicles), but I don't think I'm likely to convince you on that front with how widely the fear-based approach has been applied to the book already and our existing discussion on the topic, so unless more examination of the material is asked of me this might be my last say on the text as it currently exists.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-14T08:14:33-07:00","id":1524061,"title":"","content":"No game could survive this kind of reading. You're interpreting everything in the lowest possible way and then confidently stating that it's the only possible interpretation. CofD is meant to be a system with a lot of flexibility, I present a lot of things that CAN be small or large in scope, and you've reacted as though they can only be small in scope. I don't explicitly state that an Enthroned can be as big as Walpurgisnacht because maybe you want a smaller one. I say how they work and what they do so you can use them. This is a setting that's supposed to be completely modular and everyone's table is going to be different.<br /> <br /> I say that the Dreamlands has, among other uses, the ability to do a big world-changing story if <i>you the Storyteller</i> want to subsequently have an adventure where the world isn't changed, and explicitly frame it in terms of &quot;if you want to later tell a story that involves this change not happening.&quot; You take this to mean that the only reason to do the Dreamlands is to remove the ability to affect the world, and that the game is saying the best way to resolve things that affect the world is to do them in the Dreamlands where they don't matter, even after saying in multiple places that their use is optional. I don't think that's a remotely defensible read of the book as presented.<br /> <br /> You say that the sample Defilers are so weak that it gives the impression that they don't want anything and can't scale up, and I went back to double-check, and... no? The Violent Enforcer is explicitly a starter-level link between two types of evil. The Secretive Warlock is one of the monsters who didn't have a stat block yet, and in its description, has... the things you're saying it lacks. They're driven by fear and desperation explicitly, having always lived in fear of what others would do too them. How they change their behavior and justifications based on accumulating more power is laid out explicitly: they sought out power from an occult source or rose through the ranks of a Mystery Cult, they grow bolder and more callous by going from &quot;take only what they can justify&quot; to &quot;justify whatever you want to take.&quot; They have large amounts of minions instead of working personally through things. There's... there's nothing there to suggest they're weak and dismissible. You wrote them off as &quot;monologuing warlocks&quot; as if that meant they were not dangerous or interested in growing in power. That's a completely indefensible read. I talk directly about those specific characters seeking out and growing in power, and you cite those specific characters are giving you no reason to believe they are interested in growing in power.<br /> <br /> You say that a problem is that the explanation of the forces of Darkness only focus on the bottom rungs and the top of the ladder, and elsewhere, you essentially say there's no top of the ladder. There's the monsters of the week that're okay to kill because they don't have interiority and are used to express something about someone else's experience or personality, and there's the people who use them as tools to do what they want who do have personality and interiority. I say all over that they use the Darkness and its Darkspawn as tools to get what they want. They're the intermediate stages of the ladder. <br /> <br /> I don't think these criticisms are based in defensible readings, and that wouldn't be a problem -- I learn something from every negative criticisms even if I don't agree with it -- if these criticisms weren't the basis for ArcaneArts telling other people not to bother reading this, When Princess D / V was her favorite fangame, and the criticisms you had of Crystal were of things that were not done better in D / V. Again, that's really not cool.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28206-huitzil37","name":"Huitzil37","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-14T18:54:09-07:00","id":1524077,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Huitzil37</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1523019-princess-the-hopeful-crystal-edition?p=1524061#post1524061\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think these criticisms are based in defensible readings, and that wouldn't be a problem -- I learn something from every negative criticisms even if I don't agree with it -- if these criticisms weren't the basis for <b>ArcaneArts telling other people not to bother reading this</b>, When Princess D / V was her favorite fangame, and the criticisms you had of Crystal were of things that were not done better in D / V. Again, that's really not cool.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Huitzil37</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1523019-princess-the-hopeful-crystal-edition?p=1523273#post1523273\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> (Then seeing <b>ArcaneArts being convinced it's going to be bad and telling other people it's going to be bad without having read it is</b>... not a great thing to see.)<br /> ...<br /> I'd really, really like it if <b>ArcaneArts wasn't telling people to expect it to be bad or shallow without reading it</b>, though. That isn't nice.</div> </div> </div> </div>Uh huh.<br /> <br /> So, the comment in question.\u200b<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1523019-princess-the-hopeful-crystal-edition?p=1523254#post1523254\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The original Darkness was developed before Beast was produced. I dunno what the relationship of the writers for the First Edition was to Inferno, but I may wager they might not have read it.<br /> <br /> A smart read and write of the line between the Darkness and the Dark Mother would probably cut the line between the Dark Mother's existence as a symbol versus the Darkness having been a Pandora's Box for the various metaphysics at work in the world of Chronicles, ie, leans into it's (meta)physicality, and also draws the line that Princess originally had as a conflict point for the other monsters as effectively being creatures caught between in the shallows of the Darkness but still clawing towards the Light, particularly since the Dark Mother and her extended Families have as much a predilection to use their natures of living fear and hunger as a means to help people work out through their own darkness into a wholer actuality as they are to tear them down with it, where as the Darkness <i>only</i> has manifest interest in using a person's and people's darkness to tear them down even more.<br /> <br /> <b>Given what Satchel has said on the subject of Nightscapes, though, I wouldn't be keen to anticipate such.</b> And of course, I have no ready way to explain the possible relationships between Infernal Demons and the Darkness.</div> </div> </div> </div>And that was it.<br /> <br /> I am reminded of <a href=\"https://medium.com/maitri-for-all/the-eight-winds-cannot-move-me-a3eb1eacfbf4\" target=\"_blank\">the philosopher who claimed the eight winds couldn't move him, only to be sent scattering with a fart.</a><br /> <br /> If you were a newer creative, I would be inclined to take this moment to ask if you really want to go into anything creative at all. Handling criticism is a big part of being a creative in any capacity, and before you can even get into learning to discern whether a bit of criticism is worthwhile for what you're hoping to do and be through the creation,you first have to have a certain ability to handle negativity in general, letting it slide off of you before you even try to respond to anything. Creatives who can' handle that should probably be ready to create only for themselves-which is totally valid, mind!-because you can't get any sort of positive accolade or good ideas to build yourself up with if even a <i>bit of skepticism</i> gets you constantly reaction. If I knew nothing else about you as a creative at this time, I think the response you've provided would already be enough to indicate to me that there is little point in personally checking out your work (and there you go, an actual, negative bit of commentary).<br /> <br /> Now, I don't have to do that with you, you're a seasoned hand at fanfiction and homebrew, so I don't have to tell you that-though I will suggest that if you have any aspirations to do any creative work in the professional sphere, then clearly this is going to be something you need to take time and work on yourself about. I will say that this response casts some sharp relief on the near mythological factoid that one of the reasons there was a split in versions of Princess as resulted from the differences between you and EarthScorpion-that makes a whole more sense after this entire debacle.<br /> <br /> Where I am at now with you is instead bafflement. I don't know how you got here.<br /> <br /> It'd be one thing if I had done any actual commentary. Certainly, while I think it's misplaced with Satchel's commentary, because that has so far been a lot of good faith questioning and clarification. <b>BUT</b> it's also been a whole lot of stuff and it covers a wide array of points and issues rather than going at a strong, central point for you to turn your focus on, and that can be overwhelming for anyone. I get that.<br /> <br /> But to respond <i>three times</i> to <i>one comment</i> expressing some doubts as though I took to a crusade to tell everyone that this was trash?<br /> <br /> At this point, I think it's time I did offer some commentary, and indeed some well-aimed, discussing flaws to help criticism, that is very neccesarily going to be negative about the product, and that is this: currently, the thing that is hurting your product the most is <i>you</i>. If you are trying to promote your work, I couldn't tell what at all you think is your version's particular strong suits or appeal of play. If you're trying to get input for how it can be improved, you're not particularly engaging with someone who is taking the time to thoroughly clarify on what they're seeing as needing support.<br /> <br /> If you're trying to get people who were only going to be supportive your work, then maybe you shouldn't have privately messaged a professional in the field about how you think you resolved an issue they raised and thus brought their attention to your work. Being very specific, if you didn't want me to comment on how I felt you handled Princess (and in specific the Princess Problem*), then you shouldn't have privately messaged to directly draw my attention to what the fuck you were doing.<br /> <br /> At this point, and in this location, so far you haven't done anything that encourages people to actually read it and give it a shot. If you don't like a point of criticism, you can <i>not respond to it</i>, or if you're interested in being polite, thank people for the criticism they offered but that you will be moving on from it. The hyperbolic response you've given creates a vibe that you are insecure about your work and are unwilling to lead with that fact**, and if that's not true, then you need to change that.<br /> <br /> And I gotta say, it's disheartening to get here. I got into Princess through your early version work on it, and for a good while, I preferred it over others. But att this point, even if you hadn't wiffed it out of the gate*, I probably couldn't be talked or paid into reading your new edition.<br /> <br /> *Hutizl sent me a message drawing attention to his new edition and elaborating his thoughts on the Princess Problem, and that response was so bad it managed to fail to understand the Princess Problem twice, which convinced me that I could just skip on this edition, and for pretty much the entire time up to now, my one and only comment was going to be about that response-which I still intend to do! Spoiler: Anyone serious about trying to make Princess: the Hopeful the best it can be will know the Princess Problem is not a thing you actually have to address or even care about.<br /> **Pretty much every bit of work over in Beastland is me vibe-checking to see if my ideas are resonating with the Primordial fanbase, and I went into all of that expecting more pushback on every single point than I've gotten, so trust me when I say you can lead with &quot;I think there's probably something wrong with my take, can you help me nail down what needs improvement?&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-14T22:01:04-07:00","id":1524083,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Huitzil37</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1523019-princess-the-hopeful-crystal-edition?p=1524061#post1524061\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">CofD is meant to be a system with a lot of flexibility, I present a lot of things that CAN be small or large in scope, and you've reacted as though they can only be small in scope.</div> </div> </div> </div>Because the presentation of the information only focuses on them at the small scope or the abstract level.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't explicitly state that an Enthroned can be as big as Walpurgisnacht because maybe you want a smaller one. I say how they work and what they do so you can use them. This is a setting that's supposed to be completely modular and everyone's table is going to be different. </div> </div> </div>You say that they're the biggest threat to the biggest goal the Noble have short of eradicating the Darkness and explicitly put their global numbers in the low double-digits. You model them as a self-directed, mobile, and harder-to-kill version of Nightscapes, which themselves have a substantial amount of wordcount dedicated to them without specifying how commonplace they are. Modularity goes out the window as an excuse when these things are part of the setting's history and you have shown no problem including suggestions for adjusting Nightscape mechanics for more difficult resolution than the baseline.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I say that the Dreamlands has, among other uses, the ability to do a big world-changing story if <i>you the Storyteller</i> want to subsequently have an adventure where the world isn't changed, and explicitly frame it in terms of &quot;if you want to later tell a story that involves this change not happening.&quot; You take this to mean that the only reason to do the Dreamlands is to remove the ability to affect the world, and that the game is saying the best way to resolve things that affect the world is to do them in the Dreamlands where they don't matter, even after saying in multiple places that their use is optional. </div> </div> </div>I say the book as presented should probably not put tips for using the Dreamlands to pull your narrative punches re: world-changing stories in the main section on the Dreamlands, just as I say the place to put advice for using Shikigami as a dial for chronicle levity is probably not the Shikigami writeup itself in a fangame for a franchise whose corebooks usually have a dedicated chapter for advice on running the game.<br /> <br /> If a part of the cosmology as-written prompts in-text questions about why it matters, the text should provide meaningful answers to those questions \u2014 and on this front the &quot;why do the characters go here?&quot; section serves this function adequately, albeit the things it gestures at would do well to be supported elsewhere in the text \u2014 and it should <i>definitely</i> not have an entire extra section/sidebar questioning the point of including that part of the cosmology at all with an appeal to Comic Book Stasis and a recommendation that you use it for when you don't want to commit to the story your group is telling with the game they're playing.<br /> <br /> The text should not end a chapter <i>about</i> the world your characters' main mentors are trapped in and what can be done there by undermining the importance of that place in the game as a storytelling tool. If using the Dreamlands for a diversion is worth mentioning it is your job as a writer/developer to convey what is worthwhile about it for a game of Princess, not shrug and say &quot;use this place to delete consequences, run an AU, or play a completely different genre of game with the same system, I'm not a cop.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You say that the sample Defilers are so weak that it gives the impression that they don't want anything and can't scale up, and I went back to double-check, and... no? The Violent Enforcer is explicitly a starter-level link between two types of evil. </div> </div> </div>They're explicitly not involved in indirect threats unless they're being manipulated by a Nameless Cult masquerading as a crime syndicate. Nameless Cults are presently just described as Mystery Cults in the same paragraph as one of the text's existing &quot;people who aren't Princesses can't identify the Darkness as anything unusual&quot; bits, which means the reader has functionally nothing to go off of for that second tier beyond the Secretive Warlock; the term is presently interchangeable with any esoteric group with a Purpose, Relic, and Doctrine, and we here run into that &quot;the Darkness is and is not connected to all the rest of the supernatural&quot; problem again.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Secretive Warlock is one of the monsters who didn't have a stat block yet, and in its description, has... the things you're saying it lacks. They're driven by fear and desperation explicitly, having always lived in fear of what others would do too them. How they change their behavior and justifications based on accumulating more power is laid out explicitly: they sought out power from an occult source or rose through the ranks of a Mystery Cult, they grow bolder and more callous by going from &quot;take only what they can justify&quot; to &quot;justify whatever you want to take.&quot; </div> </div> </div>In a one-page writeup you are telling me you expect &quot;At first, they only use their powers to take what they can justify&quot; and the converse of &quot;now they know the punishment they get will be deserved&quot; to do a lot more of the heavy lifting re: sympathy for this example character than it actually does, and are apparently also expecting me to interpret a character's <i>background information</i> as reassurance that they will be a growing threat later in the chronicle instead of someone the game expects the PCs to put away before the ST has to update their character sheet.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> They have large amounts of minions instead of working personally through things. </div> </div> </div>They have a background in a Mystery Cult and work through minions, which the generalized writeup (in a paragraph that also undercuts them by emphasizing their lack of knowledge) suggests are normal people instead of either of the supernatural beings the game literally uses to model cannon-fodder minions. A Mystery Cult is not intrinsically assumed to be a large organization and neither the generalized Defiler section nor the Secretive Warlock writeup say anything about <i>large</i> numbers of minions. In the absence of a statblock the only clue about how many people answer to them is that they &quot;[rose] through a Mystery Cult,&quot; which could mean anything from &quot;leads a regional conspiracy&quot; to &quot;is a senior member of a crew of fifteen people.&quot; They could have two people and a cat doing their dirty work for all I know at this point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There's... there's nothing there to suggest they're weak and dismissible. You wrote them off as &quot;monologuing warlocks&quot; as if that meant they were not dangerous or interested in growing in power. </div> </div> </div>You end the Secretive Warlock writeup <i>literally</i> saying that they're going to start chewing the scenery once their identity is revealed and provide extremely abstracted goals. The genericized format of the Defiler writeups is doing them no favors for looking at them as characters to use as-is or as reference.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's a completely indefensible read. I talk directly about those specific characters seeking out and growing in power, and you cite those specific characters are giving you no reason to believe they are interested in growing in power. </div> </div> </div>Let me ask you: when you watch magical girl anime where a villain does not recur once dealt with, do they not generally have clear goals and things that they are doing that would make them a bigger threat to the protagonists once accomplished? Because right now the text has elements that <i>could</i> link up to present this kind of driving pressure and rather sort of <i>doesn't do anything with them</i>.<br /> <br /> The Nightscapes writeup says that certain antagonists (chiefly Defilers and Princesses of Tears) will subvert a Nightscape for their own ends, including Defilers; from the way the rest of the text focuses, apparently this doesn't happen often enough with Defilers to be worth mentioning anywhere else, whereas Lacrima interacts with the process specifically in ways that link up with a defined goal. If organized Defilers weren't stated to not know enough to recognize the common source of their power, it would be really easy to say &quot;oh, of course Dark-based Mystery Cults would try to take control of a Nightscape for [example reason to do so based on desired material outcomes or philosophical beliefs],&quot; but we also don't have much of either of the bracketed things to go on beyond the abstractions of &quot;for power&quot; and &quot;because this is the way the world works.&quot; There is presently nothing in the book pointing toward the idea of getting Defilers to give up the power of the Darkness as a practical and achievable goal for Princesses within the scope of a story, which makes the easiest way for troupes to deal with them in games where they're used to deal with them in a way that takes them out of play for the foreseeable future (whether by murder or the justice system or some other fashion); since they're NPCs, they do not gain or spend Experiences, and there is no currently published system in Princess or baseline ChroD to model the progression of evil schemes or other methods of advancement, which means they use their character sheets as-written along with whatever the Storyteller fiats onto them as the game proceeds.<br /> <br /> Deviant's conspiracies have Projects they work toward that can be hampered by players making moves against their Nodes in the Web of Pain. Mummy has sorcerers and other Arisen incentivized to pursue relics for the powers they possess or the energies that can be harvested from them. Vampire's basic kit includes an entire extended set of tools for proxifying people whose root purpose is functionally &quot;I need blood to move around and diverse investments can bring big returns on that front,&quot; and magical R&amp;D is one of those investments that can feed back into that process. The Exarchs of Mage send Mystery Commands to their Awakened servants and those humanlike monsters that have been taught to read their messages to spread the reach of the Tyrants. The Fae send the Wild Hunt in its myriad forms to drag the Lost back to Arcadia, most famously the relentless hunters with powers built to undermine their confidence in themselves and their worldview. Beasts are literally incentivized to tilt their feeding to align with their Aspirations and have access to more nuanced supernatural tools when they're well-fed enough to need at least a chapter's worth of setup for a direct meal.<br /> <br /> The list goes on. Defilers, meanwhile, are presently people with abilities modeled as Merits, drawing power from the Dark through nonspecific channels that apparently don't require an occult idiom at all, and their goals are abstractly prosaic but apparently achievable through overwhelming intimidation. Are they trying to goad local police into wandering into the Dark World with military surplus gear that they can then steal to effect a violent insurrection against the city mayor? Running an occult smear campaign against a rival business by associating its mascot with unrelenting terror? Frightening the neighbor kid into being their prom date? I could not tell you, because all the text is giving me as examples are generalities and abstractions along with the notes that the occasional idiot who volunteers for the maximum capacity of Dark power a personlike actor can have gets body-snatched by a much less functional clone. There is nothing for which pursuit of Dark magical power is necessary or even especially expedient compared to other occult methods, which means Defilers' motivations for going beyond dabbling and their means of getting there need attention and/or the Darkness needs the insidious slow-roll pointed to beyond basic gestures toward the slippery slope.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You say that a problem is that the explanation of the forces of Darkness only focus on the bottom rungs and the top of the ladder, and elsewhere, you essentially say there's no top of the ladder. There's the monsters of the week that're okay to kill because they don't have interiority and are used to express something about someone else's experience or personality, and there's the people who use them as tools to do what they want who do have personality and interiority. I say all over that they use the Darkness and its Darkspawn as tools to get what they want. They're the intermediate stages of the ladder. </div> </div> </div>The Cataphractoi writeup uses the word &quot;Darkspawn&quot; twice in its entire contents, once to say that they intrinsically have the Command Darkspawn Dread Power and once to say that they release minor Darkspawn when they finally fall apart. There is currently no example Cataphract to exemplify their use as anything resembling a Dark General instead of a Dark-tainted revenant with the nominal capacity to be more insidiously disturbing than typical Darkspawn. We are told they have the capacity to plan for the future, and then are given no real examples of what they would do with that besides &quot;defeating and devouring Princesses&quot; as a grossly inefficient means of staving off their inevitable degeneration.<br /> <br /> The general Defiler writeup, again, suggests that organized Defilers use human minions and lieutenants despite Darkspawn notionally being a better fit, and puts the only other reference to Darkspawn in the Violent Enforcer writeup, where it states that that archetype treats them like ordinary goons. The Command Darkspawn Dread Power is one of two options listed as the defining power for Defilers, and the paragraphs immediately preceding this information combined with the order of presentation tilt the priority toward Siphon Energy despite Command having direct utility for handling Darkspawn. Dark Gate is described as practically de rigueur for Defilers who don't have a reliable entrance despite a bunch of them supposedly not understanding what they're dealing with.<br /> <br /> The intermediate stages of the ladder are more than just &quot;model these things as Horrors with variable Potency, model these people as characters with extra XP options that suggest a generic organizational chain of command, and model organized groups of the latter as a Mystery Cult.&quot; The text as it currently stands is a loose assortment of tools with relatively little actionable suggestions for how to use them together in a coherent fashion beyond &quot;some of the things that are better than Darkspawn can command Darkspawn, and some of those are part of groups modeled using the generic Merit-based system for belonging to a cult.&quot;<br /> <br /> Crystal Edition has two and a half otherworlds relating to the Light and the Dark. It's a game about magical girls, specifically of the kind that fight The Darkness. In such a game, your antagonist mechanics and examples for things that are not modeled the same as PCs need to be more detailed than &quot;reference the generic mechanics from Chronicles of Darkness Core,&quot; and if they're interfacing with those otherworlds in a way that is supposed to threaten to escalate in consequences then it would be prudent to\u2026 y'know, actually have the ways they and those things interact with themselves and each other in concrete terms.<br /> <br /> Recurring bit of impossible bookkeeping notwithstanding, I know how a locus forms in Werewolf and what effect its attracting spirits of a certain type to the area implies for the neighborhood if it's not gotten under control. The lack of hard mechanics for arcane physics doesn't change the fact that I know the basic idea behind how Infrastructure works in Demon, including that it is built for a purpose whose end goal may be mysterious but whose effects can be inferred through investigation. I do not need to ask how a conspiracy in Deviant is going to advance its evil plans, because building the resources at its disposal and its operating Principles are part of the arc setup process for that game. I have almost nothing of the sort for the forces of Darkness and the war thereupon in Crystal Edition so far, because all the information given to me is in the form of loose tidbits that work serviceably enough as isolated drop-in systems but do not cohere to form a bigger picture.<br /> <br /> Nightscapes are formed at a rate that's barely gestured at from a mysterious subset of fear-based breaking points influenced in an unspecified way by the effects of the Darkness's actions in the Astral Pools of an area, and have an equally undetailed but frequent chance to form Bleeds into the Dark World around them when they shrink or expand based on yet-unstated factors. The Dark Queens supposedly dwell deep in the Dark World, and they found and enlisted the Wardens to maintain the Trap; despite the mirrored nomenclature, the Wardens have nothing to do with the Prisoners kept in Nightscapes by Nightmares, and in fact there's no statement of what the forces of Darkness that act on the Pools might be \u2014 the Dark World explicitly doesn't link up to any other part of the cosmology besides Nightscapes and the real world, so Darkspawn are out of the easy runnings, and Nightmares would seem to be the most appropriate tools if not for the lack of described interface between Nightscapes and the Dreamlands. The Enthroned are functionally mobile Nightscapes, but they number dramatically less than a hundred worldwide and their influence on things outside their confines is limited to sending out Nightmares, including the Caricature of their former Transformed self; their writeup reiterates the only semi-consistent form of influence Nightscapes have on the Dark World, which is that when they're gone the hole where the Nightscape was dumps any intruders who couldn't hold on in the Dark World.<br /> <br /> Darkspawn form in the Dark World in response to the presence of the Light, and can be commanded by Cataphractoi and some Defilers using the right Dread Power; the Dark World can be accessed by the undefined ill fortune of wandering through a Bleed or by using the Dark Gate Dread Power to turn a Bleed into a Dark Gate by taking at least three hours (Dark Gates are explicitly not naturally occurring and are &quot;incredibly hard to seal&quot; in a fashion that is not elaborated upon anywhere in the book despite Dark Gates being one of the specific example causes of Chronic Sensitivity). Defilers can and often will have the Command Darkspawn Dread Power, but apparently organized ones use the generalized garden-variety Mystery Cults they form or join as sources of minions instead; also, Defilers are one of the main culprits for Subverting a Nightscape to their own ends \u2014 what specific sort of ends are not specified, but Nightscapes feed themselves by sending out Nightmares, which collect Essence instead of Willpower and are also not Darkspawn, which leaves the reader to determine, <i>sans </i>examples, what kind of plans are being advanced specifically by scaring people out of their minds with specific traumatic fears.<br /> <br /> This is not a warped ecosystem or a hellish economy or a hideous machine of ugly impulses for my team of magical girls to work at cleaning out the nasty parts of (or even smash to bits). This is a disconnected skeleton that I'm not convinced has all the bones it needs to make whatever creature I am supposed to be being shown.<br /> <br /> It is a problem of presentation. It is a problem of the words that I have been given to read on this topic, both narrative and mechanical, and the relative quantities thereof, and the ways the parts of that text connect with each other or fail to do so. This is not asking for perfection. I do not consider &quot;have a compelling system of antagonists in a game focused on dealing with antagonists&quot; to be an unreasonable expectation, and I do not think it is unfair to say that this expectation is not being met by having a significant portion of the game's antagonist-focused wordcount dedicated to castigating the unthinking abstract power-source that makes Made-Of-Evil minions for being evil before it gives a single detailed Dark General or puts a real name to an example Defiler.<br /> <br /> I think that's plenty defensible as a read for a book you've described as currently unfinished but feature-complete.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":".  Missing line-break. 10-15-2023, 01:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-10-19T08:42:52-07:00","id":1524278,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1523019-princess-the-hopeful-crystal-edition?p=1524077#post1524077\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At this point, I think it's time I did offer some commentary, and indeed some well-aimed, discussing flaws to help criticism, that is very neccesarily going to be negative about the product, and that is this: currently, the thing that is hurting your product the most is <i>you</i>. If you are trying to promote your work, I couldn't tell what at all you think is your version's particular strong suits or appeal of play. If you're trying to get input for how it can be improved, you're not particularly engaging with someone who is taking the time to thoroughly clarify on what they're seeing as needing support.<br /> <br /> If you're trying to get people who were only going to be supportive your work, then maybe you shouldn't have privately messaged a professional in the field about how you think you resolved an issue they raised and thus brought their attention to your work. Being very specific, if you didn't want me to comment on how I felt you handled Princess (and in specific the Princess Problem*), then you shouldn't have privately messaged to directly draw my attention to what the fuck you were doing.<br /> <br /> At this point, and in this location, so far you haven't done anything that encourages people to actually read it and give it a shot. If you don't like a point of criticism, you can <i>not respond to it</i>, or if you're interested in being polite, thank people for the criticism they offered but that you will be moving on from it. The hyperbolic response you've given creates a vibe that you are insecure about your work and are unwilling to lead with that fact**, and if that's not true, then you need to change that.</div> </div> </div> </div>...Okay, what? This entire thread has been about me engaging with negative criticism and it was only over time that I went against Satchel's opinion and concluded that this was due to an inferential gap. That's not ambiguous. I am constantly talking about needing negative feedback. I responded to Satchel's negative feedback by talking about what could be improved, and only after a couple of rounds did I conclude &quot;this is never going to meet your approval.&quot; I am right now working on adding a <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1WYkYWYnynejr3T_RAUA0MVMsixYuoN8pr0TY-7KxI9c/edit\" target=\"_blank\">specific storytelling chapter</a> (poorly formatted and rambly since it hasn't even had an editing pass) that takes the parts of Satchel's criticisms I think were valid into account, and clears up a lot of what things are supposed to mean in case it wasn't obvious. I just don't expect Satchel to be satisfied with it because we're across too wide an inferential gap. I've spent the entire thread engaging with the criticisms. I was <i>so excited</i> that I could find negative feedback here because it's so god damn hard to find in any other venue, and it was crushing to realize that so much of this negative feedback wasn't actionable because it was based on taking the most negative reading possible. I've posted this in multiple other venues and got lots of positive feedback that wasn't nearly as valuable because it wasn't detailed about what needed to change. In fact, the response here has been wildly, wildly out of line with what everyone else has said in every other venue and again <i>I was happy about that</i> until it became clear this was not primarily things I could address. And even, like, people on Discord who are openly hostile to me in a way that doesn't lead to useful feedback have looked at this thread and gone &quot;yeah idk what the hell this dude's problem is.&quot;<br /> <br /> You also say that your comment wasn't actually dismissive, right before... going into detail about how dismissively hostile you are toward it, and how terrible you think it is, because a private message you never replied to was &quot;so bad&quot; and showed I didn't understand the problems at all. So you didn't read it. Because you were certain it would be so bad. And there's something about that that makes me really, genuinely angry<br /> <br /> You know I made a huge chunk of this game, right? I wasn't just there, I contributed more than just a fight with Earthscorpion. I've seen the aspects of D / V you praise and <i>I literally wrote a bunch of those.</i> But most importantly, I made Alhambra. I named Alhambra after the Alhambra Hotel in the book &quot;The Talisman,&quot; whose fantasy counterpart is a crumbling once-glorious palace where his mother, the B-Movie Queen, is dying. They're about my experience with depression, everything about that atmosphere you praised and the tangibility of its existence and that &quot;warped sort of hope through their despair&quot; you explicitly called out as great was not just the stuff I found found important to convey what it was about, it was <i>what was my own experience</i>. It's the single most important element of the setting that I needed to fix, because I was really unhappy with how they got twisted around into being about imperialism instead of sorrow. Honestly, it was a major factor in convincing me to do this re-edition. That you could talk up things I added and the atmosphere I went for and then dismiss the attempt to re-focus on that exact atmosphere and feeling and themes as if I clearly don't understand them... that's genuinely infuriating. And it's what's convinced me this is never going to result in anything but needless hostility. I had heard the OPP forums were needlessly hostile and not to expect anything good from them, and it turns out I should have listened.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"